{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: list[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if any two numbers in a list are closer to each other than a given threshold.\n\n    This function sorts the input list to efficiently find if any adjacent pair (and thus\n    any pair overall) has an absolute difference less than the specified threshold.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers (list[float]): A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold (float): The maximum absolute difference for numbers to be\n                           considered \"close\".\n                           If threshold <= 0, the condition `abs(x - y) < threshold`\n                           will always be False, as `abs(x - y)` is non-negative.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if any two distinct numbers are closer than the threshold, False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 0.5)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 2.4], 0.5)\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 1.0) # abs(2.0 - 1.0) is 1.0; 1.0 < 1.0 is False\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 1.0000001) # abs(2.0 - 1.0) is 1.0; 1.0 < 1.0000001 is True\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([], 0.1)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([5.0], 0.1)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([5.0, 5.0], 0.001) # abs(5.0 - 5.0) is 0.0; 0.0 < 0.001 is True\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([5.0, 5.0], 0.0) # abs(5.0 - 5.0) is 0.0; 0.0 < 0.0 is False\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([5.0, 6.0], -0.1) # abs(5.0 - 6.0) is 1.0; 1.0 < -0.1 is False\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Create a sorted copy of the list. This operation is O(N log N).\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # Iterate through adjacent elements. This scan is O(N).\n    # If any two elements are closer than the threshold, they must be adjacent\n    # in the sorted list or equal.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Check the absolute difference between adjacent elements\n        if abs(sorted_numbers[i] - sorted_numbers[i+1]) < threshold:\n            return True  # Found a pair of close elements\n\n    # If the loop completes, no such pair was found\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if any two numbers in a list are closer to each other than a specified threshold.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold: The maximum allowed difference for two numbers to be considered \"close\".\n\n    Returns:\n        True if there exists at least one pair of numbers (num1, num2) in the numbers list\n        such that abs(num1 - num2) < threshold. False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-4: Handle Edge Case - List Size\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # NFR-1 Performance: Sort the list. If two numbers are closer than the threshold,\n    # they must be adjacent in the sorted version of the list.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # FR-3 Core Logic: Iterate and Compare Adjacent Elements\n    # Iterate through the sorted list, comparing each element with its successor.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Since the list is sorted, sorted_numbers[i+1] is always >= sorted_numbers[i].\n        # Therefore, sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i] is equivalent to\n        # abs(sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]) for positive differences.\n        difference = sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]\n\n        # If the difference between adjacent elements is less than the threshold,\n        # we have found a pair that meets the condition.\n        if difference < threshold:\n            return True  # Found a pair closer than the threshold\n\n    # If the loop completes without returning True, no such pair exists.\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Check if in given list of numbers, are any two numbers closer to each other than\n    given threshold.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers (List[float]): A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold (float): The maximum absolute difference for numbers to be\n                           considered \\\"close\\\".\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if at least two numbers are closer than the threshold, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # REQ-FUNC-003: Handle empty or single-element list\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Sort the list to bring potentially close elements next to each other.\n    # This takes O(N log N) time and O(N) space.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-001: Iterate through adjacent elements only (O(N) operation)\n    # If any two numbers 'a' and 'b' in the original list satisfy abs(a - b) < threshold,\n    # then in the sorted list, there must be two adjacent elements x, y (where x <= y)\n    # such that abs(x - y) < threshold. This is because if x and y are not adjacent\n    # and abs(x-y) < threshold, any number z between them in the sorted list would mean\n    # abs(x-z) < abs(x-y) or abs(z-y) < abs(x-y), so an adjacent pair would also satisfy.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        num1 = sorted_numbers[i]\n        num2 = sorted_numbers[i+1]\n\n        # Calculate absolute difference and compare with threshold\n        # REQ-FUNC-004: Strict inequality check\n        if abs(num2 - num1) < threshold:\n            return True  # Close elements found (REQ-FUNC-001)\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-002: No close elements found after checking all adjacent pairs\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if, in a given list of numbers, any two numbers are closer to each other\n    than the specified threshold.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold: A positive floating-point value.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if any two numbers in the list have an absolute difference less than\n        the threshold, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.1 Business Rule: If threshold is less than or equal to 0, return False.\n    # An absolute difference cannot be strictly less than a non-positive number.\n    if threshold <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    # F.1 Business Rule: If the numbers list contains fewer than two elements,\n    # no pair can be formed, so return False.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Implement the Sorted List Approach (O(N log N) time complexity)\n    # Create a sorted copy of the numbers list. This takes O(N log N) time\n    # and O(N) space (for the copy).\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # Iterate through the sorted list, comparing only adjacent elements.\n    # If any adjacent pair has an absolute difference less than the threshold,\n    # then such a pair exists in the original list. This takes O(N) time.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Calculate the absolute difference between the current element and the next.\n        diff = abs(sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i])\n\n        # If the difference is less than the threshold, we've found a close pair.\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True  # Return True immediately\n\n    # If the loop completes, no adjacent pair (and thus no pair at all)\n    # was found to be closer than the threshold.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Check if in given list of numbers, are any two numbers closer to each other than\n    given threshold.\n\n    Optimized implementation: Sorts the list and checks adjacent elements.\n    Time complexity: O(N log N) due to sorting, where N is the number of elements.\n    Space complexity: O(N) or O(log N) depending on sort implementation.\n    \"\"\"\n    # If threshold is non-positive, no positive absolute difference can be less than it.\n    # An absolute difference of 0 (for identical numbers) is also not less than a non-positive threshold.\n    # Thus, it's impossible for `abs(num1 - num2) < threshold` to be True if threshold <= 0.\n    if threshold <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    # If there are fewer than two numbers, no pair can be closer.\n    if not numbers or len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Sort the numbers to efficiently check only adjacent elements.\n    # If any two numbers are closer than `threshold`, they must be adjacent in a sorted list.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        if abs(sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]) < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```"}
