{"task_id": "HumanEval/3", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef below_zero(operations: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Simulates bank account operations to determine if the balance ever falls below zero.\n\n    The account starts with an initial balance of zero. It processes a list of\n    financial operations (positive integers for deposits, negative for withdrawals).\n    If the balance ever drops below zero, the function immediately returns True.\n    If all operations are processed and the balance never drops below zero,\n    it returns False.\n\n    Args:\n        operations: A list of integers representing financial transactions.\n                    Positive integers are deposits, negative integers are withdrawals.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the account balance ever falls below zero during the operations,\n        False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    current_balance = 0\n    for operation in operations:\n        current_balance += operation\n        if current_balance < 0:\n            return True\n    return False\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/3", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef below_zero(operations: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Simulates bank account operations to determine if the balance ever falls below zero.\n\n    The account starts with a zero balance. Positive numbers represent deposits,\n    and negative numbers represent withdrawals.\n\n    Args:\n        operations: A list of integers representing bank transactions.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the balance ever falls below zero at any point, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize the current_balance to 0 as per F2.\n    current_balance: int = 0\n\n    # Iterate through each operation in the input list as per F3.\n    for op in operations:\n        # Update the balance by adding the current operation value (deposit or withdrawal).\n        current_balance += op\n\n        # Check if the balance has fallen below zero after the current operation as per F4.\n        if current_balance < 0:\n            # If it has, immediately return True as per F5.\n            return True\n\n    # If the loop completes without the balance ever falling below zero,\n    # return False as per F6.\n    return False\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset.\n    Mean Absolute Deviation is the average absolute difference between each\n    element and a centerpoint (mean in this case):\n    MAD = average | x - x_mean |\n    >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty for Mean Absolute Deviation calculation.\")\n\n    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n    absolute_differences = [abs(x - mean) for x in numbers]\n    \n    # If the original numbers list was non-empty, then absolute_differences will also be non-empty.\n    # Therefore, len(absolute_differences) will be > 0.\n    mad = sum(absolute_differences) / len(absolute_differences)\n    return mad\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef calculate_mad(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a given list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    The MAD is defined as the average of the absolute differences between each number\n    and the mean of the dataset.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Mean Absolute Deviation as a float. Returns 0.0 if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR.4 Empty List Handling: If the input numbers list is empty, return 0.0.\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # FR.5 Mean Calculation: Calculate the mean of the input numbers list.\n    # Count of numbers for division\n    count = len(numbers)\n    # Sum of all numbers to calculate the mean\n    sum_of_numbers = sum(numbers)\n    # Calculate the mean\n    mean = sum_of_numbers / count\n\n    # FR.6 Absolute Difference Calculation & FR.7 Average of Differences Calculation\n    # Initialize sum for absolute differences\n    sum_of_abs_diffs = 0.0\n    # Iterate through each number to calculate the absolute difference from the mean\n    for number in numbers:\n        sum_of_abs_diffs += abs(number - mean)\n\n    # Calculate MAD by averaging the absolute differences\n    mad_value = sum_of_abs_diffs / count\n\n    # FR.3 Output Format: Return the calculated MAD as a single float value.\n    return mad_value\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a list of numbers around their mean.\n\n    The Mean Absolute Deviation is defined as the average of the absolute differences\n    between each element and the mean of the dataset.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Mean Absolute Deviation as a float.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list `numbers` is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    # F.MAD.1 - Calculate Mean\n    n = len(numbers)\n    mean = sum(numbers) / n\n\n    # F.MAD.2 & F.MAD.3 - Calculate Sum of Absolute Differences\n    sum_abs_diff = 0.0\n    for number in numbers:\n        sum_abs_diff += abs(number - mean)\n\n    # F.MAD.3 - Average Absolute Differences to get MAD\n    mad = sum_abs_diff / n\n\n    return mad\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset.\n    Mean Absolute Deviation is the average absolute difference between each\n    element and a centerpoint (mean in this case):\n    MAD = average | x - x_mean |\n    >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty for Mean Absolute Deviation calculation.\")\n\n    # Step 2: Calculate Mean\n    x_mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\n    # Step 3: Calculate Absolute Deviations Sum\n    absolute_differences_sum = 0.0\n    for x in numbers:\n        absolute_differences_sum += abs(x - x_mean)\n\n    # Step 4: Calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    mad_value = absolute_differences_sum / len(numbers)\n\n    # Step 5: Return Result\n    return mad_value\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a list of numbers\n    around their mean.\n\n    MAD is the average absolute difference between each element and the mean.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Mean Absolute Deviation as a float.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list 'numbers' is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty for Mean Absolute Deviation calculation.\")\n\n    # Calculate the mean (FR.1)\n    dataset_mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\n    # Calculate the sum of absolute differences (FR.2)\n    absolute_deviations_sum = 0.0\n    for number in numbers:\n        absolute_deviations_sum += abs(number - dataset_mean)\n\n    # Calculate the Mean Absolute Deviation (FR.3)\n    mad = absolute_deviations_sum / len(numbers)\n\n    return mad\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input to MAD calculation.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[Union[int, float]]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) for a given list of numbers\n    around their mean.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of numeric types (int or float).\n\n    Returns:\n        The Mean Absolute Deviation around the mean of the dataset.\n\n    Raises:\n        InvalidInputError: If the input list is empty or contains non-numeric values.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n        1.0\n        >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1, 1, 1])\n        0.0\n        >>> mean_absolute_deviation([5])\n        0.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    # Validate that all elements are numeric\n    for num in numbers:\n        if not isinstance(num, (int, float)):\n            raise InvalidInputError(f\"All elements in the input list must be numeric. Found: {type(num)}\")\n\n    # Calculate the mean\n    sum_numbers = sum(numbers)\n    mean = sum_numbers / len(numbers)\n\n    # Calculate absolute differences from the mean\n    absolute_differences = [abs(x - mean) for x in numbers]\n\n    # Calculate MAD\n    mad = sum(absolute_differences) / len(absolute_differences)\n\n    return mad\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a given list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers whose MAD is to be computed.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Mean Absolute Deviation of the input numbers.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Input Validation: Handle empty input\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    # 2. Calculate the mean\n    count = len(numbers)\n    sum_of_numbers = sum(numbers)\n    mean = sum_of_numbers / count\n\n    # 3. Calculate sum of absolute differences\n    sum_of_absolute_differences = 0.0\n    for number in numbers:\n        sum_of_absolute_differences += abs(number - mean)\n\n    # 4. Calculate Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD)\n    mad = sum_of_absolute_differences / count\n\n    # 5. Return the result\n    return mad\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nimport re\nfrom typing import List\n\n# OutputPresenter Component\ndef display_result(mad_value: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Displays the calculated MAD value to the user, formatted to 3 decimal places.\n    (FR3.1, FR3.2)\n    \"\"\"\n    print(f\"Mean Absolute Deviation: {mad_value:.3f}\")\n\ndef display_error(message: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Displays an error message to the user.\n    (NFR3.1)\n    \"\"\"\n    print(f\"Error: {message}\")\n\n# InputReader Component\ndef get_numbers_from_user() -> List[float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Prompts the user for numbers, parses, validates, and returns them as a list of floats.\n    Handles errors by re-prompting.\n    (FR2.1, FR2.2, FR4.1)\n    \"\"\"\n    while True:\n        user_input = input(\"Enter numbers separated by spaces or commas (e.g., 1.0 2.5 3.0): \")\n        \n        # Replace commas with spaces, then normalize multiple spaces to single spaces, and strip leading/trailing.\n        cleaned_input = re.sub(r'[,\\\\s]+', ' ', user_input).strip()\n        \n        if not cleaned_input:\n            # If input is empty after cleaning, return an empty list.\n            # The main loop will then handle the FR4.2 requirement (empty set).\n            return [] \n\n        str_numbers = cleaned_input.split(' ')\n        numbers: List[float] = []\n        try:\n            for s_num in str_numbers:\n                numbers.append(float(s_num))\n            return numbers\n        except ValueError:\n            # FR4.1: If parsing to float fails, display error and re-prompt.\n            display_error(\"Invalid input. Please enter only numeric values.\")\n            # Loop continues, prompting user again.\n\n# MADCalculator Component\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) for a list of numbers.\n    (FR1, FR4.2, FR4.3)\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR4.2: If list is empty, MAD is 0.0.\n    # FR4.3: If list contains only one number, MAD is 0.0.\n    if not numbers or len(numbers) == 1:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # FR1.1: Calculate mean\n    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\n    # FR1.2: Calculate absolute differences\n    absolute_differences = [abs(x - mean) for x in numbers]\n\n    # FR1.3, FR1.4: Calculate average of absolute differences (MAD)\n    mad = sum(absolute_differences) / len(absolute_differences)\n    return mad\n\n# Main Application Flow\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"\n    Controls the main application loop, orchestrating input, calculation, and output.\n    \"\"\"\n    while True:\n        try:\n            numbers = get_numbers_from_user() # Calls InputReader component\n            \n            if not numbers: # FR4.2: Handling empty input after validation\n                display_error(\"No numbers entered. MAD cannot be calculated for an empty set.\")\n                # Ask user if they want to try again, then continue or break.\n                choice = input(\"Would you like to try again? (yes/no): \").strip().lower()\n                if choice == 'yes':\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    break\n\n            mad_value = mean_absolute_deviation(numbers) # Calls MADCalculator component\n            display_result(mad_value) # Calls OutputPresenter component\n\n        except Exception as e: # NFR2.2: Generic error handler for unexpected issues\n            display_error(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\")\n\n        # Ask user if they want to perform another calculation\n        choice = input(\"Calculate another MAD? (yes/no): \").strip().lower()\n        if choice != 'yes':\n            break\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, status, Request\nfrom fastapi.exceptions import RequestValidationError\nfrom fastapi.responses import JSONResponse\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\nfrom typing import List, Union\nimport logging\n\n# Configure basic logging for internal server errors\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.ERROR, format='% (asctime)s - % (levelname)s - % (message)s')\n\n# The core calculation function for Mean Absolute Deviation\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) for a list of floating-point numbers.\n    If the list is empty, returns 0.0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # Calculate the mean of the numbers\n    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\n    # Calculate the sum of absolute differences from the mean\n    sum_absolute_differences = sum(abs(x - mean) for x in numbers)\n\n    # Calculate MAD\n    mad = sum_absolute_differences / len(numbers)\n    return mad\n\n# Initialize the FastAPI application\napp = FastAPI(\n    title=\"Mean Absolute Deviation Calculator\",\n    description=\"API for calculating the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a list of numbers.\",\n    version=\"1.0.0\"\n)\n\n# Pydantic model for the request body\nclass MadRequest(BaseModel):\n    numbers: List[Union[float, int]] = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"A list of floating-point or integer numbers for MAD calculation.\",\n        example=[1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]\n    )\n\n# Pydantic model for a successful response\nclass MadResponse(BaseModel):\n    mad: float = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"The calculated Mean Absolute Deviation.\",\n        example=1.0\n    )\n\n# Pydantic model for an error response\nclass ErrorResponse(BaseModel):\n    error: str = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"A descriptive error message.\",\n        example=\"Invalid input type. Expected a list of numbers.\"\n    )\n\n# Custom exception handler for Pydantic validation errors (RequestValidationError)\n# This allows returning HTTP 400 Bad Request with a custom error format as per SRS.\n@app.exception_handler(RequestValidationError)\nasync def validation_exception_handler(request: Request, exc: RequestValidationError):\n    errors = exc.errors()\n    detail_msg = \"Invalid request payload.\"\n\n    if errors:\n        # Check for specific error types to match SRS examples\n        first_error = errors[0]\n        loc = first_error.get(\"loc\", ())\n        error_type = first_error.get(\"type\")\n        input_value = first_error.get(\"input\")\n\n        # Case 1: 'numbers' field itself is not a list\n        if len(loc) == 1 and loc[0] == \"numbers\" and error_type == \"list_type\":\n            detail_msg = \"Invalid input type. Expected 'numbers' to be a list.\"\n        # Case 2: An element within the 'numbers' list is not a number\n        elif len(loc) == 2 and loc[0] == \"numbers\" and error_type in [\"float_type\", \"int_type\", \"number_type\"]:\n            detail_msg = f\"Invalid list element: '{input_value}' is not a number.\"\n        else:\n            # Fallback for other validation errors\n            # Can provide more detailed Pydantic error messages if needed for debugging\n            detail_msg = f\"Invalid request payload: {first_error.get('msg')}\"\n\n    return JSONResponse(\n        status_code=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST,\n        content=ErrorResponse(error=detail_msg).dict()\n    )\n\n# API endpoint for calculating Mean Absolute Deviation\n@app.post(\n    \"/calculate-mad\",\n    response_model=MadResponse,\n    responses={\n        status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST: {\"model\": ErrorResponse, \"description\": \"Bad Request\"},\n        status.HTTP_500_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR: {\"model\": ErrorResponse, \"description\": \"Internal Server Error\"}\n    },\n    summary=\"Calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\",\n    description=\"Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) for a given list of numerical values.\"\n)\nasync def calculate_mad_endpoint(request: MadRequest):\n    \"\"\"\n    Accepts a JSON payload with a 'numbers' list and returns the calculated MAD.\n    Performs input validation and handles errors gracefully.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Pydantic automatically validates the request body against MadRequest model.\n        # If validation fails, RequestValidationError is raised and handled by the custom handler.\n\n        # Convert all numbers to float before passing to the core calculation function\n        # to ensure consistency and prevent potential issues with mixed types.\n        numbers_as_floats = [float(n) for n in request.numbers]\n\n        calculated_mad = mean_absolute_deviation(numbers_as_floats)\n\n        return MadResponse(mad=calculated_mad)\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Catch any other unexpected internal errors during the calculation process\n        logging.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred during MAD calculation: {e}\", exc_info=True)\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=status.HTTP_500_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,\n            detail=ErrorResponse(error=\"An internal server error occurred.\").dict()\n        )\n\n# To run this application:\n# 1. Save it as, e.g., `main.py`\n# 2. Install FastAPI and Uvicorn: `pip install fastapi uvicorn pydantic`\n# 3. Run from your terminal: `uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000`\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\n# mad_calculator.py\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input scenarios.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef calculate_mean(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the arithmetic mean of a list of numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The mean of the numbers.\n\n    Raises:\n        InvalidInputError: If the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Cannot calculate mean of an empty list.\")\n    return sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) for a given list of numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Mean Absolute Deviation as a float.\n\n    Raises:\n        InvalidInputError: If the input is not a list or contains non-numeric elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Basic type validation for the list and its elements\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list) or not all(isinstance(x, (int, float)) for x in numbers):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a list of numbers, and all elements must be numeric.\")\n\n    # Handle empty list as per SRS F1 Error Handling: return 0.0\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0.0\n    \n    # Calculate the mean of the numbers\n    mean_val = calculate_mean(numbers)\n    \n    # Calculate the absolute differences from the mean\n    absolute_differences = [abs(x - mean_val) for x in numbers]\n    \n    # Calculate the mean of these absolute differences\n    mad_value = calculate_mean(absolute_differences)\n    \n    return mad_value\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a specified integer delimiter between every two consecutive elements\n    of an input list of integers.\n\n    The function creates and returns a new list, leaving the original `numbers`\n    list unchanged.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers (List[int]) to be processed.\n        delimeter: An integer (int) to insert between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers (List[int]) with the delimiter interspersed.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 0)\n        [1, 0, 2, 0, 3]\n        >>> intersperse([], 0)\n        []\n        >>> intersperse([1], 0)\n        [1]\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list: List[int] = []\n\n    # Handle edge cases as per design document\n    if not numbers:\n        return result_list\n    if len(numbers) == 1:\n        result_list.append(numbers[0])\n        return result_list\n\n    # General case: iterate and intersperse\n    for i, num in enumerate(numbers):\n        result_list.append(num)\n        # Append delimiter if it's not the last element\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result_list.append(delimeter)\n\n    return result_list\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a specified delimiter between consecutive elements of a list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: The input list of integers.\n        delimeter: The integer to be inserted between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list with the delimiter interspersed between original elements.\n        If the input list is empty, an empty list is returned.\n        If the input list contains one element, that list is returned unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result_list = []\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        result_list.append(numbers[i])\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result_list.append(delimeter)\n\n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Insert a number 'delimeter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers'\n    >>> intersperse([], 4)\n    []\n    >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n    [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) == 0:\n        return []\n    if len(numbers) == 1:\n        return numbers[:]  # Return a new list containing the single element\n\n    result: List[int] = []\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        result.append(numbers[i])\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:  # If not the last element\n            result.append(delimeter)\n            \n    return result\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a given delimiter number between every two consecutive elements\n    of an input list of numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n        delimeter: An integer to be inserted between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers with the delimiter interspersed.\n        Returns an empty list if the input 'numbers' list is empty.\n        Returns the original list's single element if 'numbers' contains only one.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list = []\n\n    # FR-002: Empty Input List Handling\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    # FR-003: Single Element Input List Handling\n    if len(numbers) == 1:\n        return [numbers[0]]\n\n    # FR-001: Intersperse Delimiter (General Case)\n    # Iterate through the input list using an index\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        # Append the current element from the original list\n        result_list.append(numbers[i])\n        \n        # If it's not the last element, append the delimiter\n        # The delimiter is inserted *between* elements, not after the last one.\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result_list.append(delimeter)\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimiter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a number 'delimiter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers`.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers (List[int]): The input list of integers.\n        delimiter (int): The integer to insert between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A new list with the delimiter interspersed.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> intersperse([], 4)\n        []\n        >>> intersperse([1], 4)\n        [1]\n        >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n        [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    if len(numbers) == 1:\n        return [numbers[0]]\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        result.append(numbers[i])\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result.append(delimiter)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Insert a number 'delimeter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers`.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers (List[int]): The input list of integers.\n        delimeter (int): The integer to be inserted as a delimiter.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A new list with the delimiter interspersed.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> intersperse([], 4)\n        []\n        >>> intersperse([1], 99)\n        [1]\n        >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n        [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n        >>> intersperse([10, 20], 0)\n        [10, 0, 20]\n        >>> intersperse([-1, -2, -3], -99)\n        [-1, -99, -2, -99, -3]\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    if not numbers:  # Handles empty list as per FR-2\n        return result\n\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        result.append(numbers[i])\n        # Insert delimiter only if it's not the last element\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n            \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a specified integer delimiter between consecutive elements of an input\n    list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers (List[int]) to be interspersed.\n        delimeter: An integer (int) to be inserted between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers (List[int]) with the delimiter inserted.\n        - If the input 'numbers' list is empty, returns an empty list.\n        - If the input 'numbers' list contains a single element, returns a new list\n          containing only that single element.\n        - The original 'numbers' list is not modified.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n        [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n        >>> intersperse([], 4)\n        []\n        >>> intersperse([1], 4)\n        [1]\n        >>> intersperse([10, 20], 0)\n        [10, 0, 20]\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[int] = []\n    for i, num in enumerate(numbers):\n        result.append(num)\n        # Append the delimiter only if it's not the last element\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a specified delimiter between consecutive elements of a given list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers to intersperse.\n        delimeter: An integer value to be inserted between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list with the delimiter interspersed between the original numbers.\n        Returns an empty list if the input 'numbers' list is empty.\n        Returns a list containing the original single element if 'numbers' has one element.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n\n    # FR.3: Handle empty input list\n    if not numbers:\n        return result\n\n    # Iterate through the numbers list to build the result list\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        # Append the current number\n        result.append(numbers[i])\n        \n        # FR.4: Insert delimiter if it's not the last element\n        # FR.5: Ensures no delimiter is inserted after the last element or for a single element list\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n            \n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a delimeter between consecutive elements of a list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: The input list of integers.\n        delimeter: The integer value to be inserted.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing the original numbers with the delimeter\n        inserted between consecutive elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    num_elements = len(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(num_elements):\n        current_num = numbers[i]\n        result.append(current_num)\n\n        # Append delimeter only if it's not the last element\n        if i < num_elements - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimiter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a given delimiter integer between every two consecutive elements of an input list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: The input list of integers.\n        delimiter: The integer value to be inserted into the list.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing the original elements interspersed with the delimiter.\n        Returns an empty list if the input `numbers` list is empty.\n        Returns a list with the single element if `numbers` contains only one element.\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[int] = []\n\n    # Handle Edge Cases:\n    # a. If numbers is an empty list, return result (which is []).\n    if not numbers:\n        return result\n    \n    # b. If numbers contains only one element, append numbers[0] to result\n    #    and then return result. This handles the case where no delimiters are needed.\n    if len(numbers) == 1:\n        result.append(numbers[0])\n        return result\n\n    # Process General Case (len(numbers) > 1):\n    # a. Append the first element of numbers (numbers[0]) to result.\n    result.append(numbers[0])\n\n    # b. Iterate through the rest of the numbers list, starting from the second element (index 1)\n    #    up to len(numbers) - 1. Let the current element be current_num.\n    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):\n        # i. Append the delimiter to result.\n        result.append(delimiter)\n        # ii. Append current_num (which is numbers[i]) to result.\n        result.append(numbers[i])\n            \n    # Return the result list.\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef _calculate_max_depth(group_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum nesting depth for a single string of parentheses.\n    Assumes the input group_string contains only '(' and ')' characters\n    and is free of spaces.\n    \"\"\"\n    current_depth = 0\n    max_depth = 0\n\n    for char in group_string:\n        if char == '(':\n            current_depth += 1\n            max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n        elif char == ')':\n            current_depth -= 1\n            # For well-formed parentheses, current_depth won't go negative before\n            # the end of the string.\n            \n    return max_depth\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Analyzes a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses,\n    separated by spaces, and determines the deepest level of nesting\n    for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string containing groups of parentheses separated by spaces.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers, where each integer is the maximum nesting level\n        of a respective group in the order they appear in the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_depths = []\n    \n    # Split the input string into groups.\n    # Using split() without arguments handles multiple spaces as a single delimiter\n    # and removes empty strings from the result. However, `paren_string.split(' ')`\n    # will keep empty strings. The design explicitly mentions `paren_string.split(' ')`\n    # and then `group_str.strip()` check.\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n\n    for group_str in groups:\n        # Skip empty strings or strings containing only whitespace.\n        if not group_str.strip():\n            continue\n        \n        # Calculate the maximum depth for the current group and append to results.\n        depth = _calculate_max_depth(group_str)\n        result_depths.append(depth)\n        \n    return result_depths\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef _get_max_nesting_level(group_str: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the deepest nesting level for a single parenthetical group string.\n    It increments the current level for each '(' and decrements for each ')'.\n    The maximum level is the highest current_level reached. Other characters are ignored.\n\n    Args:\n        group_str (str): A string representing a single group of parentheses.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The maximum nesting level found within the group_str. Returns 0 for an empty string.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_level = 0\n    current_level = 0\n    for char in group_str:\n        if char == '(':\n            current_level += 1\n            max_level = max(max_level, current_level)\n        elif char == ')':\n            current_level -= 1\n            # Note: current_level can go negative if there are more closing than opening parentheses\n            # at any point, but max_level only captures the highest positive depth.\n    return max_level\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing multiple space-separated groups of nested parentheses\n    and returns a list of the maximum nesting levels for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string (str): The input string, which may contain multiple groups of\n                            nested parentheses separated by single spaces.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: An ordered list of integers, where each integer represents the\n                   maximum nesting level for the corresponding group. An empty group\n                   (e.g., resulting from consecutive spaces or a group being \"\")\n                   shall have a nesting level of 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    results: List[int] = []\n\n    # FR1.1 - Group Separation: Split the string by a single space character.\n    # This behavior means that consecutive spaces will result in empty strings ('')\n    # in the 'group_strings' list, and each such empty string is treated as a group.\n    # For example, \"a  b\".split(' ') will yield [\"a\", \"\", \"b\"].\n    group_strings = paren_string.split(' ')\n\n    # FR1.0 - Input Handling: The function accepts a single string argument.\n    # FR1.3 - Output Generation: The system shall return a list of integers.\n    for group_str in group_strings:\n        # FR1.2 - Maximum Nesting Level Calculation for each identified group.\n        # FR1.2.4 - An empty group will yield a max_level of 0, as _get_max_nesting_level handles this.\n        max_depth = _get_max_nesting_level(group_str)\n        results.append(max_depth)\n\n    return results\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Analyzes a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses and\n    determines the deepest level of nesting for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string consisting of one or more groups of parentheses,\n                      separated by spaces. Each group contains only '(' and ')'.\n\n    Returns:\n        A List of integers, where each integer corresponds to the deepest nesting\n        level of a group in the order they appear in the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list: List[int] = []\n    \n    # Split the input string by spaces. str.split() without arguments handles\n    # multiple spaces between groups and leading/trailing spaces correctly,\n    # and ensures no empty strings are produced if only spaces are present.\n    groups = paren_string.split()\n\n    for group_string in groups:\n        # If the group string is empty after splitting (e.g., due to specific split behavior,\n        # though str.split() generally prevents this with default arguments), skip it.\n        if not group_string:\n            continue\n\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n\n        for char in group_string:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n            # According to FR-6 and the constraints, input will only contain '(' and ')'\n            # and spaces. So, no other characters need explicit handling here.\n        \n        result_list.append(max_depth)\n\n    return result_list\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses, separated by spaces,\n    and determines the deepest level of nesting for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string where multiple groups of nested parentheses are separated by spaces.\n                      It is assumed that each group is well-formed and only contains '(', ')', and space characters.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers, where each integer is the deepest nesting level for the\n        corresponding parenthesis group in the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_depths = []\n    \n    # FR.1: Parse Input String by splitting it into individual parenthesis groups\n    group_strings = paren_string.split(' ')\n\n    # Iterate through each group string to calculate its maximum nesting depth\n    for group_string in group_strings:\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth_for_group = 0\n\n        # FR.2: Calculate Nesting Depth for each identified parenthesis group\n        for char in group_string:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                # Update max_depth_for_group if current depth is higher\n                max_depth_for_group = max(max_depth_for_group, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n            # Assuming only '(', ')', and ' ' characters as per requirements,\n            # no explicit handling for other characters is needed within a group.\n        \n        # FR.3: Aggregate Results\n        result_depths.append(max_depth_for_group)\n        \n    # FR.4: Return Results\n    return result_depths"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Analyzes a string containing multiple space-separated groups of nested parentheses\n    and determines the deepest level of nesting for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string (str): A string containing '(', ')', and space characters.\n                            Groups of parentheses are separated by spaces.\n                            Each group is assumed to be well-formed and balanced.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of integers, where each integer represents the maximum\n                   nesting depth for the corresponding group in the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n    results = []\n\n    for group_str in groups:\n        if not group_str:\n            # Skip empty strings resulting from multiple spaces or leading/trailing spaces\n            continue\n\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth_for_group = 0\n\n        for char in group_str:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                max_depth_for_group = max(max_depth_for_group, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n            # Assuming input only contains '(', ')', and space as per SRS.\n            # No other characters need explicit handling.\n\n        results.append(max_depth_for_group)\n\n    return results"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the deepest nesting level for each space-separated group\n    of nested parentheses in the input string.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string (str): A string containing space-separated nested \n                            parentheses groups, e.g., '(()()) ((()))'.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of integers, where each integer represents the \n                   deepest nesting level for the corresponding group.\n                   Returns an empty list if the input string is empty or \n                   contains only spaces.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> parse_nested_parens('(()()) ((())) () ((())()())')\n        [2, 3, 1, 3]\n        >>> parse_nested_parens('')\n        []\n        >>> parse_nested_parens('   ')\n        []\n        >>> parse_nested_parens('()')\n        [1]\n    \"\"\"\n    result_depths: List[int] = []\n    \n    # Split the input string into individual groups.\n    # str.split() with no arguments handles multiple spaces and leading/trailing spaces correctly,\n    # and also effectively handles empty input strings or strings with only spaces by returning an empty list.\n    groups = paren_string.split() \n\n    for group_str in groups:\n        # group_str will not be empty here due to how str.split() without args works.\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n        \n        for char in group_str:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n            # Per requirements, we assume input within groups are '(' or ')'.\n            # The calculation implicitly handles potential malformed groups by\n            # simply tracking the highest depth reached, consistent with \"deepest level\".\n\n        result_depths.append(max_depth)\n\n    return result_depths\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Analyzes a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses and\n    determines the deepest level of nesting for each group.\n\n    Each group of parentheses is assumed to be well-formed and balanced.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string (str): A string containing space-separated groups of\n                            nested parentheses, e.g., '(()()) ((())) ()'.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list where each integer corresponds to the maximum\n                   nesting depth of its respective group, in the order\n                   they appeared in the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    results: List[int] = []\n\n    # FR2: Group Separation\n    # Split the input string by spaces. Filter out any empty strings that might\n    # result from multiple spaces or leading/trailing spaces.\n    groups = [g for g in paren_string.split(' ') if g]\n\n    # FR3: Nesting Depth Calculation\n    # Iterate through each individual group of parentheses\n    for group_str in groups:\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n\n        # Iterate through characters of the current group to calculate depth\n        for char in group_str:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                # Update max_depth if the current nesting level is deeper\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n            # As per requirements, we assume valid parentheses and do not\n            # explicitly handle other characters or malformed cases.\n        \n        # Add the maximum depth found for the current group to the results list\n        results.append(max_depth)\n\n    # FR4: Output Format\n    return results\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Input to this function is a string represented multiple groups for nested parentheses separated by spaces.\n    For each of the group, output the deepest level of nesting of parentheses.\n    E.g. (()()) has maximum two levels of nesting while ((())) has three.\n\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('(()()) ((())) () ((())()())')\n    [2, 3, 1, 3]\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('')\n    []\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('()')\n    [1]\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('((()))')\n    [3]\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('() ()')\n    [1, 1]\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('    ')\n    []\n    >>> parse_nested_parens(' (()) ') # Leading/trailing spaces on string and group\n    [2]\n    \"\"\"\n    all_max_depths: List[int] = []\n    \n    # Split the input string into individual groups.\n    # str.split() without arguments handles multiple spaces and filters out empty strings.\n    groups: List[str] = paren_string.split() \n\n    for group_str in groups:\n        # Calculate the nesting depth for the current group\n        current_depth: int = 0\n        max_depth: int = 0\n\n        for char in group_str:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n            # Update max_depth after each character, capturing the peak.\n            # This handles cases like `(())`, where current_depth goes 1, 2, 1, 0,\n            # ensuring max_depth captures 2.\n            max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n        \n        all_max_depths.append(max_depth)\n        \n    return all_max_depths\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Analyzes a string containing multiple space-separated groups of nested parentheses\n    and determines the maximum nesting level within each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string where groups of parentheses are separated by spaces.\n                      Example: \"(()()) ((())) ()\"\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers, where each integer represents the maximum nesting depth\n        for a corresponding group in the input string.\n        Example: [2, 3, 1] for \"(()()) ((())) ()\"\n                 [0, 0, 0] for \"  \" (empty groups or groups with only spaces)\n                 [] for \"\" (empty input string)\n    \"\"\"\n    def _calculate_max_depth_for_group(group_string: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Internal helper function to calculate the maximum nesting depth for a\n        single string of parentheses.\n\n        Args:\n            group_string: A single string representing a group of parentheses.\n\n        Returns:\n            The maximum nesting depth found within group_string. Returns 0 if\n            the string is empty or contains no parentheses.\n        \"\"\"\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n        for char in group_string:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                # As per REQ-FUN-008, groups are assumed to be well-formed.\n                # If they are not, current_depth might go negative, but max_depth\n                # correctly tracks the maximum positive depth achieved.\n                current_depth -= 1\n        return max_depth\n\n    if not paren_string:\n        return []\n\n    result_depths: List[int] = []\n    # Split by one or more spaces. `split(' ')` handles multiple spaces by\n    # inserting empty strings into the list, which our helper function\n    # correctly processes as 0 depth.\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n\n    for group_str in groups:\n        depth = _calculate_max_depth_for_group(group_str)\n        result_depths.append(depth)\n\n    return result_depths\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/7", "solution": "```python\n# File: string_processing_logic.py\n\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef filter_by_substring(strings: List[str], substring: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters an input list of strings, returning only those that contain the given substring.\n    The filtering is case-sensitive.\n\n    Args:\n        strings: A list of strings to filter.\n        substring: The substring to look for.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing only the strings that contain the substring.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Robustness checks for direct function calls, though Pydantic handles much of this upstream.\n    if not isinstance(strings, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'strings' must be a list.\")\n    if not all(isinstance(s, str) for s in strings):\n        raise TypeError(\"All items in 'strings' list must be strings.\")\n    if not isinstance(substring, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'substring' must be a string.\")\n\n    return [s for s in strings if substring in s]\n\n```"}
