{"task_id": "HumanEval/8", "solution": "```python\ndef sum_product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    # Initialize sum to 0 (additive identity)\n    current_sum: int = 0\n    \n    # Initialize product to 1 (multiplicative identity)\n    current_product: int = 1\n    \n    # Iterate through each number in the input list\n    for number in numbers:\n        current_sum += number        # Add the number to the running sum\n        current_product *= number    # Multiply the number with the running product\n        \n    # Return the final sum and product as a tuple\n    return (current_sum, current_product)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the rolling maximum of a list of integers.\n\n    Returns a new list where each element at index `i` represents the maximum \n    value found in the input list `numbers` from index `0` up to and \n    including index `i`.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers representing the rolling maximums.\n        Returns an empty list if the input list is empty.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n        [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n        >>> rolling_max([])\n        []\n        >>> rolling_max([5])\n        [5]\n        >>> rolling_max([10, 5, 20, 3, 15])\n        [10, 10, 20, 20, 20]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result = []\n    current_max = float('-inf') # Initialize with a very small number\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        current_max = max(current_max, num)\n        result.append(current_max)\n        \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes a new list where each element represents the maximum value encountered\n    so far in the input list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers where result[i] is the maximum of numbers[0]\n        through numbers[i]. Returns an empty list if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[int] = []\n\n    if not numbers:\n        return result\n\n    current_max: int = numbers[0]\n    result.append(current_max)\n\n    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):\n        num = numbers[i]\n        if num > current_max:\n            current_max = num\n        result.append(current_max)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the rolling maximum for a given list of integers.\n\n    The rolling maximum at any point is the largest number encountered\n    in the sequence up to and including that point.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers representing the rolling maximums.\n        The output list will have the same length as the input list.\n        If the input list is empty, an empty list is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list: List[int] = []\n    \n    # Initialize current_max with negative infinity.\n    # This ensures that the first actual number encountered in the list\n    # will correctly become the initial current_max.\n    # Python's max() function handles comparison between float('-inf') and int correctly,\n    # and the result will be an int once the first element is processed.\n    current_max = float('-inf') \n\n    # Handle the empty list edge case as per FR-002.\n    # If the list is empty, the loop will not execute, and an empty result_list\n    # will be returned, which is correct.\n    if not numbers:\n        return result_list\n\n    # Iterate through each number in the input list.\n    # This loop runs N times, contributing to O(N) time complexity (NFR-001).\n    for number in numbers:\n        # Update current_max to be the maximum of its current value\n        # and the number currently being processed.\n        # This implements FR-001: result[i] = max(numbers[0], ..., numbers[i]).\n        current_max = max(current_max, number)\n        \n        # Append the updated current_max to the result list.\n        # This builds a new list, satisfying FR-003 (not modifying input, returning new list).\n        # Appending to result_list N times results in O(N) space complexity (NFR-002).\n        # Since current_max will always be an integer after the first non-empty input,\n        # no explicit cast to int is needed here for Python's dynamic typing,\n        # as the type will resolve to int from the input numbers.\n        result_list.append(current_max)\n\n    return result_list\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n    >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n    [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list: List[int] = []\n    current_max: int | float = float('-inf')\n\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        current_max = max(current_max, number)\n        result_list.append(int(current_max))\n\n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers (List[int]): The input list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A new list where each element at index `i` is the maximum value\n                   found in `numbers` from index `0` up to `i`.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n        [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n        >>> rolling_max([])\n        []\n        >>> rolling_max([5])\n        [5]\n        >>> rolling_max([-1, -5, 0, 2, -3])\n        [-1, -1, 0, 2, 2]\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    current_max = None\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        if current_max is None or number > current_max:\n            current_max = number\n        result.append(current_max)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the rolling maximum of a list of integers.\n\n    For each element at index i, the rolling maximum is the maximum value\n    from index 0 up to and including index i.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing the rolling maximum elements.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n        [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n        >>> rolling_max([])\n        []\n        >>> rolling_max([5])\n        [5]\n        >>> rolling_max([10, 5, 20, 15])\n        [10, 10, 20, 20]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result_list: List[int] = []\n    current_max: int = numbers[0]\n\n    result_list.append(current_max) # First element is its own rolling max\n\n    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):\n        current_value = numbers[i]\n        current_max = max(current_max, current_value)\n        result_list.append(current_max)\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes a list where each element represents the maximum value encountered\n    up to that point in the input sequence of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integer numbers for which the rolling maximum\n                 is to be calculated.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers, where each element is the maximum value\n        encountered up to that point in the input sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result: List[int] = []\n    current_max: int = numbers[0]\n    result.append(current_max)\n\n    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):\n        num = numbers[i]\n        current_max = max(current_max, num)\n        result.append(current_max)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the rolling maximum of a list of integers.\n\n    For a given list of integers, generates a new list where each element\n    is the maximum value found from the beginning of the sequence up to\n    the current moment.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers representing the rolling maximums.\n        Returns an empty list if the input list is empty.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n        [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n        >>> rolling_max([])\n        []\n        >>> rolling_max([5])\n        [5]\n        >>> rolling_max([10, 5, 12, 3, 8])\n        [10, 10, 12, 12, 12]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize the list to store rolling maximums\n    rolling_max_list: List[int] = []\n    \n    # Initialize current_max with the first element\n    # This handles the case where all numbers are negative or single-element lists\n    current_max: int = numbers[0]\n    rolling_max_list.append(current_max)\n\n    # Iterate from the second element\n    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):\n        num = numbers[i]\n        if num > current_max:\n            current_max = num\n        rolling_max_list.append(current_max)\n\n    return rolling_max_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the rolling maximum of a list of integers.\n\n    For each element at index i in the input list, the corresponding element\n    in the output list is the maximum value among all elements from the\n    beginning of the input list up to and including numbers[i].\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers where each element is the rolling maximum\n        up to that point in the input list. Returns an empty list if the\n        input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result: List[int] = []\n    current_max: int = numbers[0]\n    result.append(current_max)\n\n    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):\n        current_max = max(current_max, numbers[i])\n        result.append(current_max)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the shortest palindrome that begins with the supplied string.\n\n    Algorithm:\n    1. Find the longest postfix of the supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    2. Append to the end of the supplied string the reverse of the string prefix\n       that comes before the identified palindromic suffix.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The starting string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The shortest palindrome starting with the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    reversed_prefix_to_append = \"\"\n\n    # Iterate i from 0 to len(string) - 1.\n    # string[i:] is a potential palindromic postfix.\n    # The first 'i' for which string[i:] is a palindrome identifies\n    # the longest such postfix because 'i' starts from 0 and increments.\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        current_postfix = string[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(current_postfix):\n            # If string[i:] is a palindrome, then string[:i] is the prefix\n            # whose reverse needs to be appended.\n            prefix_to_reverse = string[:i]\n            reversed_prefix_to_append = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n            break # Found the longest palindromic postfix, so exit loop\n\n    # The result is the original string concatenated with the reversed prefix.\n    # Handles empty string naturally: if string is \"\", loop does not run,\n    # reversed_prefix_to_append remains \"\", result is \"\" + \"\" = \"\".\n    return string + reversed_prefix_to_append\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return s == s[::-1]\n\ndef make_palindrome(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Transforms an input string into the shortest possible palindrome\n    that starts with the original string.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The shortest palindrome string that begins with the supplied input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Handle Empty String (FR4)\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n\n    longest_palindromic_postfix_start_index = len(s) # Default to full string if already palindrome, or no suffix found\n\n    # Step 2: Find Longest Palindromic Postfix (FR3, part 1)\n    # Iterate from the beginning of the string to find the starting index\n    # of the longest palindromic postfix.\n    for i in range(len(s)):\n        current_postfix = s[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(current_postfix):\n            longest_palindromic_postfix_start_index = i\n            break # The first one found (from left) will be the longest\n\n    # Step 3: Extract Prefix to Reverse\n    # This is the part of the string before the longest palindromic postfix.\n    prefix_to_reverse = s[:longest_palindromic_postfix_start_index]\n\n    # Step 4: Reverse the Prefix\n    reversed_prefix = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n\n    # Step 5: Construct Result (FR2)\n    # Concatenate the original string with the reversed prefix.\n    result = s + reversed_prefix\n    \n    # FR5 (Already Palindrome Handling) is implicitly covered:\n    # If s is already a palindrome, longest_palindromic_postfix_start_index will be 0.\n    # prefix_to_reverse will be '', reversed_prefix will be '', and result will be s + '' = s.\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the shortest palindrome starting with the input string.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The shortest palindrome string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if is_palindrome(string):\n        return string\n\n    n = len(string)\n    # Iterate through possible start points for the palindromic postfix\n    # i represents the length of the prefix that needs to be reversed and appended.\n    # The actual prefix is string[:i], and the postfix is string[i:].\n    # We are looking for the *longest* palindromic postfix, which implies the *shortest* prefix\n    # that needs to be reversed. This happens when we find the first 'i' (smallest 'i')\n    # for which string[i:] is a palindrome.\n    for i in range(n):\n        postfix = string[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(postfix):\n            # The part of the string that needs to be reversed is the prefix before\n            # the identified palindromic postfix.\n            prefix_to_reverse = string[:i]\n            reversed_prefix = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n            return string + reversed_prefix\n    \n    # This part of the code should theoretically not be reached for non-empty strings,\n    # because at minimum, a single character postfix (string[n-1:]) will always be a palindrome.\n    # For empty strings, the initial check handles it.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n\n    Args:\n        string (str): The string to check.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\ndef make_palindrome(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Transforms an input string into a palindrome by identifying its longest\n    palindromic postfix and appending the reversed prefix that precedes it.\n\n    Args:\n        s (str): The input string to be transformed.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The resulting palindrome string.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR6: Handle empty string\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # FR2: Find Longest Palindromic Postfix\n    # Iterate through the string to find the starting index of the longest\n    # palindromic postfix.\n    # The loop finds the first (leftmost) 'i' such that s[i:] is a palindrome.\n    # Because 'i' starts from 0, s[i:] will be longest when 'i' is smallest\n    # for a palindrome, and we are looking for the *longest* palindromic postfix,\n    # so we want the *first* 'i' (smallest 'i') that gives a palindrome.\n    #\n    # Correction: The design says \"The *first* `postfix` found to be a palindrome\n    # (when `i` starts from `0` and increases) will be the *longest* palindromic\n    # postfix because we are checking suffixes in decreasing order of length\n    # (starting with the full string `s[0:]`).\" This implies checking from left\n    # to right (i from 0 to len-1) will give the longest suffix *that is a palindrome*.\n    # Let's verify this logic.\n    # Example: 'google'\n    # i=0, postfix='google' - not pal\n    # i=1, postfix='oogle' - not pal\n    # i=2, postfix='ogle' - not pal\n    # i=3, postfix='gle' - not pal\n    # i=4, postfix='le' - not pal\n    # i=5, postfix='e' - is pal. Here, i_start_of_palindrome = 5.\n    # The longest palindromic postfix is indeed 'e'.\n    #\n    # Example: 'madam'\n    # i=0, postfix='madam' - is pal. Here, i_start_of_palindrome = 0.\n    # The longest palindromic postfix is 'madam'.\n    #\n    # This logic from the design document is correct.\n    start_of_palindromic_postfix_index = 0\n    for i in range(len(s)):\n        postfix = s[i:]\n        # FR7: Utilize is_palindrome function\n        if is_palindrome(postfix):\n            start_of_palindromic_postfix_index = i\n            break # Found the longest palindromic postfix\n\n    # FR3: Identify Prefix Identification\n    # The part of the string before the longest palindromic postfix.\n    prefix_to_reverse = s[:start_of_palindromic_postfix_index]\n\n    # FR4: Prefix Reversal\n    reversed_prefix = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n\n    # FR5: Result Construction\n    # Concatenate the original string with the reversed prefix.\n    result = s + reversed_prefix\n\n    # Return Result\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Query, HTTPException, status\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom typing import Optional\nfrom fastapi.exceptions import RequestValidationError\nfrom fastapi.responses import JSONResponse\nfrom starlette.exceptions import HTTPException as StarletteHTTPException\n\n# --- Palindrome Logic Module ---\n\ndef is_palindrome(text: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n    A palindrome is a string that reads the same forwards and backwards.\n    \"\"\"\n    return text == text[::-1]\n\ndef make_palindrome(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates the shortest palindrome that begins with the supplied string.\n    Algorithm:\n    1. Find the smallest index 'k' such that the suffix text[k:] is a palindrome.\n       This effectively finds the longest palindromic suffix of the input string.\n    2. The prefix that needs to be reversed and appended is text[:k].\n    3. The shortest palindrome is constructed as text + (prefix_to_reverse[::-1]).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not text:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Find the smallest 'k' such that text[k:] is a palindrome\n    k = 0\n    for i in range(len(text)):\n        if is_palindrome(text[i:]):\n            k = i\n            break\n    \n    prefix_to_reverse = text[:k]\n    return text + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n\n# --- FastAPI Application ---\n\napp = FastAPI(\n    title=\"Palindrome Service\",\n    description=\"A microservice for checking and generating palindromes.\",\n    version=\"1.0.0\",\n)\n\n# --- Configuration ---\nMAX_STRING_LENGTH = 10000 # As per SRS for performance/security\n\n# --- Pydantic Models for Request/Response ---\n\nclass PalindromeRequest(BaseModel):\n    text: str\n\nclass PalindromeCheckResponse(BaseModel):\n    input_string: str\n    is_palindrome: bool\n\nclass PalindromeMakeResponse(BaseModel):\n    input_string: str\n    shortest_palindrome: str\n\nclass ErrorResponse(BaseModel):\n    error: str\n    status_code: int\n\n# --- Helper for input validation (length) ---\ndef check_string_length(text: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the input string exceeds the maximum allowed length.\n    Raises HTTPException if the length is exceeded.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(text) > MAX_STRING_LENGTH:\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST,\n            detail={\"error\": f\"Input string exceeds maximum length of {MAX_STRING_LENGTH} characters.\", \"status_code\": 400}\n        )\n\n# --- Custom Exception Handlers for consistent error responses ---\n\n@app.exception_handler(RequestValidationError)\nasync def validation_exception_handler(request, exc: RequestValidationError):\n    \"\"\"\n    Handles Pydantic/FastAPI validation errors (e.g., missing parameters, type mismatches)\n    and formats them according to the SRS (HTTP 400 Bad Request with specific messages).\n    \"\"\"\n    error_message = \"Invalid input parameters.\"\n    is_text_error_handled = False\n\n    for error in exc.errors():\n        loc = error.get(\"loc\", ())\n        error_type = error.get(\"type\")\n\n        if \"text\" in loc:\n            if loc[0] == \"query\":\n                if error_type == \"value_error.missing\":\n                    error_message = \"Query parameter 'text' is required.\"\n                elif error_type == \"string_type\":\n                    error_message = \"Query parameter 'text' must be a string.\"\n                is_text_error_handled = True\n                break # Prioritize 'text' field errors\n            elif loc[0] == \"body\":\n                if error_type == \"value_error.missing\":\n                    error_message = \"JSON body with 'text' field is required.\"\n                elif error_type == \"string_type\":\n                    error_message = \"Field 'text' in JSON body must be a string.\"\n                is_text_error_handled = True\n                break # Prioritize 'text' field errors\n        elif \"body\" in loc and len(loc) == 1 and error_type == \"value_error.missing\":\n            # This covers cases where the entire JSON body is missing for POST, not just 'text' field\n            error_message = \"JSON body with 'text' field is required.\"\n            is_text_error_handled = True\n            break\n    \n    # If no specific 'text' field error was found but other validation errors exist\n    if not is_text_error_handled and exc.errors():\n        error_message = \"Invalid request body format or value type.\"\n\n\n    return JSONResponse(\n        status_code=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST,\n        content={\"error\": error_message, \"status_code\": 400},\n    )\n\n@app.exception_handler(StarletteHTTPException)\nasync def http_exception_handler(request, exc: StarletteHTTPException):\n    \"\"\"\n    Handles HTTPExceptions raised by the application, including custom ones\n    (e.g., from check_string_length). Ensures consistent error response format.\n    \"\"\"\n    # If the detail is already a dict with \"error\" and \"status_code\", use it directly.\n    if isinstance(exc.detail, dict) and \"error\" in exc.detail and \"status_code\" in exc.detail:\n        return JSONResponse(\n            status_code=exc.status_code,\n            content=exc.detail,\n        )\n    # Otherwise, format a generic error response.\n    return JSONResponse(\n        status_code=exc.status_code,\n        content={\"error\": str(exc.detail), \"status_code\": exc.status_code},\n    )\n\n# --- API Endpoints ---\n\n@app.get(\n    \"/api/palindrome/check\",\n    response_model=PalindromeCheckResponse,\n    summary=\"Check if a string is a palindrome (GET)\",\n    responses={\n        status.HTTP_200_OK: {\"model\": PalindromeCheckResponse},\n        status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST: {\"model\": ErrorResponse}\n    }\n)\nasync def check_palindrome_get(\n    text: str = Query(..., description=f\"The string to check for palindrome. Max length: {MAX_STRING_LENGTH} characters.\")\n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the provided string (from query parameter) is a palindrome.\n    \"\"\"\n    check_string_length(text)\n    is_pal = is_palindrome(text)\n    return PalindromeCheckResponse(input_string=text, is_palindrome=is_pal)\n\n@app.post(\n    \"/api/palindrome/check\",\n    response_model=PalindromeCheckResponse,\n    summary=\"Check if a string is a palindrome (POST)\",\n    responses={\n        status.HTTP_200_OK: {\"model\": PalindromeCheckResponse},\n        status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST: {\"model\": ErrorResponse}\n    }\n)\nasync def check_palindrome_post(request_body: PalindromeRequest):\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the provided string (in JSON body) is a palindrome.\n    \"\"\"\n    check_string_length(request_body.text)\n    is_pal = is_palindrome(request_body.text)\n    return PalindromeCheckResponse(input_string=request_body.text, is_palindrome=is_pal)\n\n@app.get(\n    \"/api/palindrome/make\",\n    response_model=PalindromeMakeResponse,\n    summary=\"Generate shortest palindrome (GET)\",\n    responses={\n        status.HTTP_200_OK: {\"model\": PalindromeMakeResponse},\n        status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST: {\"model\": ErrorResponse}\n    }\n)\nasync def make_palindrome_get(\n    text: str = Query(..., description=f\"The string to use as a prefix for the shortest palindrome. Max length: {MAX_STRING_LENGTH} characters.\")\n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates the shortest palindrome that starts with the supplied string (from query parameter).\n    \"\"\"\n    check_string_length(text)\n    shortest_pal = make_palindrome(text)\n    return PalindromeMakeResponse(input_string=text, shortest_palindrome=shortest_pal)\n\n@app.post(\n    \"/api/palindrome/make\",\n    response_model=PalindromeMakeResponse,\n    summary=\"Generate shortest palindrome (POST)\",\n    responses={\n        status.HTTP_200_OK: {\"model\": PalindromeMakeResponse},\n        status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST: {\"model\": ErrorResponse}\n    }\n)\nasync def make_palindrome_post(request_body: PalindromeRequest):\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates the shortest palindrome that starts with the supplied string (in JSON body).\n    \"\"\"\n    check_string_length(request_body.text)\n    shortest_pal = make_palindrome(request_body.text)\n    return PalindromeMakeResponse(input_string=request_body.text, shortest_palindrome=shortest_pal)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n    A string is a palindrome if it reads the same forwards and backward.\n    The comparison is case-sensitive and includes all characters.\n\n    Args:\n        s (str): The string to be checked.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return s == s[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the shortest palindrome that begins with the supplied string.\n\n    The algorithm identifies the longest suffix of the supplied string that is\n    itself a palindrome. The prefix of the string that comes before this\n    suffix is then reversed and appended to the original string to form\n    the shortest palindrome.\n\n    Args:\n        s (str): The initial string.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The shortest palindrome starting with 's'.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-11: make_palindrome('') SHALL return ''\n    # Handle the base case: If the string is already a palindrome, return it as is.\n    if is_palindrome(s):\n        return s\n\n    prefix_to_reverse = \"\"\n    # FR-10.1: Identify the longest postfix (suffix) of the supplied string that is itself a palindrome.\n    # Iterate from the beginning of the string to find the starting index 'i'\n    # of the longest palindromic suffix. The first suffix found to be a palindrome\n    # by iterating 'i' from 0 will correspond to the longest such suffix\n    # that allows us to form the shortest palindrome by prepending characters.\n    for i in range(len(s)):\n        current_suffix = s[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(current_suffix):\n            # FR-10.2: Determine the prefix of the supplied string that comes *before* this longest palindromic suffix.\n            prefix_to_reverse = s[:i]\n            break\n            \n    # FR-10.3: Reverse the identified prefix.\n    reversed_prefix = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n\n    # FR-10.4: Append the reversed prefix to the original supplied string to form the result.\n    return s + reversed_prefix\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\n\"\"\"\nA Python module providing utility functions related to palindromes.\n\nThis module includes functions to check if a string is a palindrome and to\nconstruct the shortest palindrome that begins with a given string.\n\"\"\"\n\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines whether the input string is a palindrome.\n\n    A palindrome is a sequence of characters that reads the same forwards and\n     backwards. The comparison is case-sensitive, and all characters\n    (alphanumeric, spaces, punctuation, etc.) are considered significant.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The string to be evaluated.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates the shortest possible palindrome that begins with the supplied\n    input string.\n\n    The algorithm finds the longest palindromic postfix of the input string.\n    It then takes the prefix of the original string that comes before this\n    palindromic postfix, reverses it, and appends it to the original string\n    to form the shortest palindrome.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The initial string to extend into a palindrome.\n\n    Returns:\n        The shortest palindrome that starts with the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    n = len(string)\n    \n    # Iterate through all possible postfixes of the input string,\n    # starting from the longest (the string itself) down to the shortest (a single character).\n    # 'i' represents the starting index of the current postfix.\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_postfix = string[i:]\n        \n        # Check if the current postfix is a palindrome.\n        if is_palindrome(current_postfix):\n            # This 'current_postfix' is the longest palindromic postfix\n            # because we are iterating from i=0 (longest postfix first).\n            \n            # The 'prefix_to_reverse' is the part of the original string\n            # that comes before this longest palindromic postfix.\n            prefix_to_reverse = string[:i]\n            \n            # Reverse this prefix.\n            reversed_prefix = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n            \n            # Concatenate the original string with the reversed prefix to\n            # form the shortest palindrome.\n            return string + reversed_prefix\n    \n    # This part of the code should theoretically be unreachable for non-empty strings,\n    # as a single-character postfix (string[n-1:]) is always a palindrome,\n    # ensuring that a palindromic postfix will always be found.\n    # However, as a failsafe or if a string had an unexpected property,\n    # one might consider a default, but the algorithm ensures a return inside the loop.\n    # The return within the loop is guaranteed to execute.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "import os\nimport logging\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\n# Configure logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format='%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s')\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\napp.config['JSONIFY_PRETTYPRINT_REGULAR'] = False # For more compact JSON responses\n\n# --- Core Logic Module ---\ndef is_palindrome(text: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a string is a palindrome.\"\"\"\n    # Note: Input validation for type is handled at API layer.\n    return text == text[::-1]\n\ndef make_palindrome(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Generates the shortest palindrome starting with the input string.\n    \n    This function finds the longest suffix of the input string that is\n    already a palindrome. The portion of the string *before* this suffix\n    is then reversed and appended to the original string to form the\n    shortest palindrome.\n    \n    Examples:\n    - \"cat\" -> \"catac\" (longest palindromic suffix is \"t\", prefix to reverse \"ca\")\n    - \"madam\" -> \"madam\" (longest palindromic suffix is \"madam\", prefix to reverse \"\")\n    - \"race\" -> \"racecar\" (longest palindromic suffix is \"e\", prefix to reverse \"rac\")\n    - \"\" -> \"\"\n    \"\"\"\n    # Note: Input validation for type is handled at API layer.\n    if not text:\n        return \"\"\n\n    longest_palindromic_suffix = \"\"\n    # Iterate from the start of the string (index i) to get suffixes s[i:].\n    # The first suffix found that is a palindrome will be the longest one.\n    for i in range(len(text)):\n        suffix = text[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(suffix):\n            longest_palindromic_suffix = suffix\n            break\n    \n    # The part of the string before the longest palindromic suffix needs to be reversed\n    # and appended to the original string.\n    prefix_to_reverse = text[:len(text) - len(longest_palindromic_suffix)]\n    \n    return text + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n\n# --- API Layer ---\n\ndef validate_text_input(data: dict) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates the 'text' field in the request body.\n    Returns an error message string if invalid, otherwise None.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not data:\n        return \"Request body is empty or malformed JSON.\"\n    text = data.get('text')\n    if text is None:\n        return \"'text' field is required.\"\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        return \"'text' field must be a string.\"\n    \n    # Allow empty strings as valid input, as core logic handles them meaningfully.\n    # E.g., is_palindrome(\"\") -> True, make_palindrome(\"\") -> \"\"\n    \n    return None # Input is valid\n\n@app.route('/api/v1/palindrome/check', methods=['POST'])\ndef check_palindrome_api():\n    \"\"\"\n    Endpoint to check if a given string is a palindrome.\n    \"\"\"\n    logger.info(f\"Received request for /api/v1/palindrome/check from {request.remote_addr}\")\n    try:\n        data = request.get_json()\n        error_message = validate_text_input(data)\n        if error_message:\n            logger.warning(f\"Bad Request: {error_message} for /api/v1/palindrome/check. Data: {data}\")\n            return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Invalid input: {error_message}\"}), 400\n        \n        input_text = data['text']\n        result = is_palindrome(input_text)\n        \n        response_data = {\n            \"original_text\": input_text,\n            \"is_palindrome\": result\n        }\n        logger.info(f\"Successfully processed check_palindrome for '{input_text}'. Result: {result}\")\n        return jsonify(response_data), 200\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.exception(f\"Internal Server Error during check_palindrome_api: {e}\")\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"An unexpected error occurred.\"}), 500\n\n@app.route('/api/v1/palindrome/make', methods=['POST'])\ndef make_palindrome_api():\n    \"\"\"\n    Endpoint to generate the shortest palindrome starting with the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    logger.info(f\"Received request for /api/v1/palindrome/make from {request.remote_addr}\")\n    try:\n        data = request.get_json()\n        error_message = validate_text_input(data)\n        if error_message:\n            logger.warning(f\"Bad Request: {error_message} for /api/v1/palindrome/make. Data: {data}\")\n            return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Invalid input: {error_message}\"}), 400\n        \n        input_text = data['text']\n        result = make_palindrome(input_text)\n        \n        response_data = {\n            \"original_text\": input_text,\n            \"shortest_palindrome\": result\n        }\n        logger.info(f\"Successfully processed make_palindrome for '{input_text}'. Result: {result}\")\n        return jsonify(response_data), 200\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.exception(f\"Internal Server Error during make_palindrome_api: {e}\")\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"An unexpected error occurred.\"}), 500\n\n@app.route('/health', methods=['GET'])\ndef health_check():\n    \"\"\"\n    Endpoint for health checks.\n    \"\"\"\n    logger.debug(f\"Received health check request from {request.remote_addr}\")\n    return jsonify({\"status\": \"healthy\", \"service\": \"palindrome-service\"}), 200\n\n# --- Configuration and Run ---\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # Get port from environment variable, default to 5000\n    port = int(os.environ.get('PORT', 5000))\n    # Determine debug mode from environment variable\n    debug_mode = os.environ.get('FLASK_DEBUG', 'False').lower() in ('true', '1', 't')\n    \n    if debug_mode:\n        app.debug = True\n        logger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n        logger.debug(f\"Running in debug mode. Port: {port}\")\n    else:\n        app.debug = False\n        logger.info(f\"Running in production mode. Port: {port}\")\n\n    # Run the Flask app\n    # host='0.0.0.0' makes the server accessible externally, necessary for containerized deployment\n    app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=port)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\n# palindrome_core.py\nclass PalindromeCore:\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_palindrome(input_string: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n        Case-sensitive. An empty string is considered a palindrome.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(input_string, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n        return input_string == input_string[::-1]\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def make_palindrome(input_string: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Generates the shortest palindrome that begins with the supplied input string.\n        Case-sensitive. An empty string returns an empty string.\n        If the input is already a palindrome, it returns the original string.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(input_string, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n        if not input_string:\n            return \"\"\n\n        # As per SRS/SDD requirement SVC-PAL-002.5\n        if PalindromeCore.is_palindrome(input_string):\n            return input_string\n\n        n = len(input_string)\n        \n        # Find the longest palindromic suffix.\n        # This is done by iterating from the beginning of the string (index 0)\n        # and checking progressively shorter suffixes. The first suffix found\n        # that is a palindrome will be the longest one.\n        longest_palindromic_suffix_start_index = n \n        \n        for i in range(n):\n            suffix = input_string[i:]\n            if PalindromeCore.is_palindrome(suffix):\n                longest_palindromic_suffix_start_index = i\n                break \n\n        # The part of the string before the longest palindromic suffix needs to be reversed\n        # and appended to the original string to form the shortest palindrome.\n        prefix_to_reverse = input_string[0:longest_palindromic_suffix_start_index]\n        \n        # Reverse the prefix\n        reversed_prefix = prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n        \n        # Concatenate original string with the reversed prefix\n        return input_string + reversed_prefix\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n\n    A palindrome is a sequence of characters that reads the same forwards and backward.\n    This check is case-sensitive and sensitive to spaces/special characters.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The string to check.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the shortest palindrome by extending the input string according to a specific algorithm.\n\n    The algorithm is:\n    1. Identify the longest postfix of the input string that is also a palindrome.\n    2. Identify the prefix of the input string that remains BEFORE this longest\n       palindromic postfix.\n    3. Reverse this remaining prefix.\n    4. Prepend the reversed prefix to the original input string.\n\n    Note: This algorithm results in the original string being a suffix of the\n    generated palindrome, not necessarily a prefix.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The shortest palindrome constructed using the specified algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n    # If the string is empty, it's already a palindrome.\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # If the string is already a palindrome, return it as is.\n    # This also handles single-character strings as they are palindromes.\n    if is_palindrome(string):\n        return string\n\n    # Find the longest palindromic postfix.\n    # Iterate from the beginning of the string to find the starting index\n    # of the longest palindromic postfix. The first one found (smallest `i`)\n    # will correspond to the longest postfix.\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        postfix = string[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(postfix):\n            # 'postfix' is the longest palindromic postfix.\n            # 'string[:i]' is the remaining prefix.\n            remaining_prefix = string[:i]\n            reversed_prefix = remaining_prefix[::-1]\n            return reversed_prefix + string\n    \n    # This line should technically be unreachable because a single-character postfix\n    # (the last character of the string) is always a palindrome, ensuring the loop\n    # will always find a match at `i = len(string) - 1` at the latest.\n    return \"\"\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/11", "solution": "```python\ndef string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs a bitwise exclusive OR (XOR) operation on two binary strings.\n\n    This function takes two binary strings, `a` and `b`, and computes their\n    bitwise XOR. The result is returned as a new binary string.\n    Input strings are expected to be of equal length and contain only '0's and '1's.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first binary string. Expected to contain only '0's and '1's.\n        b: The second binary string. Expected to contain only '0's and '1's,\n           and be of the same length as `a`.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new binary string representing the result of the XOR operation.\n        The length of the returned string will be equal to the length of the\n        input strings.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If input strings are not of equal length, or if they\n                    contain characters other than '0' or '1'.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> string_xor('010', '110')\n        '100'\n        >>> string_xor('111', '000')\n        '111'\n        >>> string_xor('1010', '0101')\n        '1111'\n        >>> string_xor('', '')\n        ''\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input strings must be of equal length.\")\n\n    # Validate that input strings contain only '0's and '1's\n    valid_chars = {'0', '1'}\n    if not all(char in valid_chars for char in a):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input string 'a' must contain only '0's and '1's.\")\n    if not all(char in valid_chars for char in b):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input string 'b' must contain only '0's and '1's.\")\n\n    result_chars = []\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        char_a = a[i]\n        char_b = b[i]\n\n        # XOR logic: '0' if chars are the same, '1' if different\n        if char_a == char_b:\n            result_chars.append('0')\n        else:\n            result_chars.append('1')\n\n    return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/11", "solution": "```python\ndef string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input are two strings a and b consisting only of 1s and 0s.\n    Perform binary XOR on these inputs and return result also as a string.\n    >>> string_xor('010', '110')\n    '100'\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input strings must have the same length.\")\n\n    if not all(char in '01' for char in a) or not all(char in '01' for char in b):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input strings must contain only '0' or '1' characters.\")\n\n    result_chars = []\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        int_a = int(a[i])\n        int_b = int(b[i])\n        xor_int = int_a ^ int_b\n        result_chars.append(str(xor_int))\n\n    return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```"}
