{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    This function adheres to the following rules:\n    - GCD(a, b) = GCD(abs(a), abs(b)).\n    - If one input is zero and the other is a non-zero integer x, the GCD is abs(x).\n    - If both inputs are zero, the GCD is 0.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of a and b, always as a non-negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.REQ.GCD.1.2: Calculate the GCD using the absolute values of the inputs.\n    x = abs(a)\n    y = abs(b)\n\n    # F.REQ.GCD.1.3: Handle zero inputs.\n    # F.REQ.GCD.1.3.1: If one input is zero and the other is a non-zero integer x, the GCD shall be abs(x).\n    # F.REQ.GCD.1.3.2: If both inputs are zero, the GCD shall be 0.\n    # If x is 0, GCD(0, y) = y. If y is also 0, this returns 0.\n    if x == 0:\n        return y\n    # If y is 0, GCD(x, 0) = x. This case is only reached if x was not 0.\n    if y == 0:\n        return x\n\n    # F.REQ.GCD.1.4: Implement a mathematically sound algorithm (Euclidean algorithm).\n    # NFR.PERF.1: Efficient time complexity (logarithmic).\n    # NFR.REL.1: Accuracy.\n    while y:\n        x, y = y, x % y\n    \n    # F.REQ.GCD.1.5: The component shall return a single non-negative integer.\n    return x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the\n    Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    Handles positive, negative, and zero inputs according to the following rules:\n    - For positive integers a and b, the GCD is the largest positive integer\n      that divides both a and b without leaving a remainder.\n    - If one input is zero and the other is a non-zero integer x, the GCD is |x|.\n    - If both inputs are zero, the GCD is 0.\n    - If one or both inputs are negative, the GCD is calculated as the GCD of\n      their absolute values.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle negative inputs by taking absolute values.\n    # This also correctly covers GCD(x, 0) and GCD(0, 0) according to requirements.\n    a_abs = abs(a)\n    b_abs = abs(b)\n\n    # Euclidean algorithm (iterative version)\n    # GCD(a, b) = GCD(b, a % b)\n    # The base case is when b becomes 0, then GCD(a, 0) = a.\n    while b_abs != 0:\n        remainder = a_abs % b_abs\n        a_abs = b_abs\n        b_abs = remainder\n    \n    return a_abs\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    The GCD is the largest positive integer that divides both 'a' and 'b' without leaving a remainder.\n    This function handles positive, negative, and zero inputs according to standard mathematical conventions:\n    - If one input is zero and the other is non-zero, the GCD is the absolute value of the non-zero input (e.g., gcd(5, 0) = 5).\n    - If both inputs are zero, the GCD is 0 (e.g., gcd(0, 0) = 0).\n    - Negative integer inputs are treated as their absolute values for GCD calculation (e.g., gcd(-6, 9) = gcd(6, 9) = 3).\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of 'a' and 'b'.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n        1\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(6, 9)\n        3\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 7)\n        7\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(-10, 15)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(-12, -18)\n        6\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 0)\n        0\n    \"\"\"\n    a = abs(a)\n    b = abs(b)\n\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    \n    return a\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "def greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    :param a: The first integer.\\n\n    :param b: The second integer.\\n\n    :return: The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n    1\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\n    a = abs(a)\n    b = abs(b)\n\n    while b != 0:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "def greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    Handles positive, negative, and zero inputs according to mathematical definitions.\n    The returned GCD is always non-negative.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.REQ.GCD.004: Handle negative inputs by taking absolute values.\n    # The returned GCD shall always be non-negative.\n    current_a = abs(a)\n    current_b = abs(b)\n\n    # F.REQ.GCD.003: Handle Both Zero Inputs.\n    # If both inputs are zero, the GCD is defined as 0.\n    if current_a == 0 and current_b == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # F.REQ.GCD.002: Handle Zero Inputs (one non-zero, one zero).\n    # If one input is zero and the other is non-zero, the GCD is the absolute value\n    # of the non-zero integer.\n    if current_a == 0:\n        return current_b\n    if current_b == 0:\n        return current_a\n\n    # F.REQ.GCD.001: Calculate GCD for Positive Integers (after normalization).\n    # Use the iterative Euclidean algorithm (modulo variant).\n    while current_b != 0:\n        remainder = current_a % current_b\n        current_a = current_b\n        current_b = remainder\n    \n    return current_a"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the GCD.\n    It handles positive, negative, and zero inputs according to standard mathematical conventions:\n    - gcd(a, b) = gcd(|a|, |b|)\n    - gcd(a, 0) = |a|\n    - gcd(0, 0) = 0\n\n    Args:\n        a (int): The first integer.\n        b (int): The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n        1\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(10, 0)\n        10\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 7)\n        7\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 0)\n        0\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(-25, 15)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(-10, -5)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(12, 18)\n        6\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Handle special case for gcd(0, 0)\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Step 2: Take Absolute Values\n    # This addresses FR6 (Handling Negative Inputs) and ensures the Euclidean algorithm\n    # can operate on non-negative values, as well as FR8 (Handling Zero Inputs (One))\n    # where the result is the absolute value of the non-zero integer.\n    a = abs(a)\n    b = abs(b)\n\n    # Step 3: Implement Euclidean Algorithm (Iterative Approach)\n    # The loop continues as long as b is not zero.\n    # In each iteration, a becomes the old b, and b becomes the remainder of the old a divided by the old b.\n    while b != 0:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    \n    # Step 4: Return Result\n    # When b becomes 0, a holds the GCD of the original absolute values.\n    return a\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    The function handles zero and negative inputs according to standard mathematical conventions:\n    - GCD(0, 0) is 0.\n    - GCD(n, 0) or GCD(0, n) is |n|.\n    - For non-zero integers, the GCD is the largest positive integer that divides both numbers\n      without a remainder. Negative inputs are handled by operating on their absolute values\n      to ensure a positive GCD result.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = abs(a)\n    y = abs(b)\n\n    if x == 0 and y == 0:\n        return 0\n    if x == 0:\n        return y\n    if y == 0:\n        return x\n\n    while y != 0:\n        # Standard Euclidean algorithm step\n        temp = y\n        y = x % y\n        x = temp\n    \n    return x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" Return the greatest common divisor of two integers a and b\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n    1\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n    5\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(10, 0)\n    10\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 0)\n    0\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(-6, 9)\n    3\n    \"\"\"\n    x = abs(a)\n    y = abs(b)\n\n    # FR1.6: If both inputs are zero, the GCD shall be 0.\n    if x == 0 and y == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR1.5: If one input is zero and the other is non-zero, the GCD shall be the absolute value of the non-zero input.\n    # The Euclidean algorithm handles this implicitly:\n    # If y is 0 initially, loop is skipped, x (which is |a|) is returned.\n    # If x is 0 initially and y is non-zero, the first iteration sets x=y, y=0. Then x (which is |b|) is returned.\n\n    # Euclidean Algorithm (Iterative)\n    while y != 0:\n        x, y = y, x % y\n    \n    return x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    The GCD is always a non-negative integer.\n    - gcd(x, 0) returns abs(x)\n    - gcd(0, x) returns abs(x)\n    - gcd(0, 0) returns 0\n\n    Args:\n        a (int): The first integer.\n        b (int): The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The greatest common divisor, always non-negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.5: Handle negative inputs by taking absolute values.\n    # This ensures the GCD is always non-negative and simplifies subsequent steps.\n    a = abs(a)\n    b = abs(b)\n\n    # F.4.3: Handle the special case gcd(0, 0).\n    # If both inputs are 0 after taking absolute values, return 0.\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # F.1, F.4.1, F.4.2: Implement the iterative Euclidean algorithm.\n    # This loop continues as long as b is not zero.\n    # When b becomes zero, a holds the GCD.\n    while b != 0:\n        # The remainder of a divided by b becomes the new b.\n        # The old b becomes the new a.\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    \n    # F.3: Return the final value of a, which is the greatest common divisor.\n    # It will always be non-negative due to the initial abs() operations.\n    return a\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "```python\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the\n    Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    This function adheres to standard mathematical definitions, treating negative\n    inputs as their absolute values for the purpose of GCD computation and\n    handling zero inputs as follows:\n    - GCD(x, 0) returns abs(x)\n    - GCD(0, y) returns abs(y)\n    - GCD(0, 0) returns 0\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    a_abs = abs(a)\n    b_abs = abs(b)\n\n    while b_abs != 0:\n        remainder = a_abs % b_abs\n        a_abs = b_abs\n        b_abs = remainder\n\n    return a_abs\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/14", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef all_prefixes(string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates a list of all prefixes of a given input string,\n    ordered from shortest to longest.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The input string for which prefixes are to be generated.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of strings, where each element is a prefix of the input string,\n        ordered by length in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    prefixes_list: List[str] = []\n\n    # Iterate from 1 up to the length of the string (inclusive).\\n    # 'i' represents the desired length of the current prefix.\\n    for i in range(1, len(string) + 1):\n        # Extract the prefix using string slicing.\\n        # string[0:i] extracts characters from index 0 up to i-1.\\n        current_prefix = string[0:i]\n        \n        # Append the current prefix to the list.\\n        prefixes_list.append(current_prefix)\n\n    return prefixes_list\n```"}
