{"task_id": "HumanEval/17", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_music(music_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string representing musical notes and converts it into a list of\n    integers, where each integer represents the duration in beats for the\n    corresponding note.\n\n    The input string contains space-separated musical note representations.\n    The function maps specific note representations to their beat values:\n    - 'o' (whole note): 4 beats\n    - 'o|' (half note): 2 beats\n    - '.|' (quarter note): 1 beat\n\n    The parsing is robust to leading/trailing spaces and multiple spaces\n    between notes. The order of notes is preserved.\n\n    Args:\n        music_string (str): A string containing space-separated musical note\n                            representations. E.g., 'o o| .| o|'\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of integers, where each integer is the beat duration\n                   for the corresponding note. E.g., [4, 2, 1, 2]\n\n    Example:\n        >>> parse_music('o o| .| o| o| .| .| .| .| o o')\n        [4, 2, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Define the mapping from note string representations to their beat values\n    note_to_beats_map = {\n        'o': 4,   # Whole note\n        'o|': 2,  # Half note\n        '.|': 1   # Quarter note\n    }\n\n    # Pre-process the input string:\n    # 1. Remove leading/trailing whitespace using .strip().\n    # 2. Split the string by single spaces. This operation naturally handles\n    #    multiple spaces by producing empty strings in the resulting list,\n    #    which will be filtered out.\n    note_tokens = music_string.strip().split(' ')\n\n    # Initialize an empty list to store the parsed beat values\n    beats_list: List[int] = []\n\n    # Iterate through each token and map it to its beat value\n    for token in note_tokens:\n        # Skip any empty strings that might result from multiple spaces\n        # (e.g., \"o  o|\" splits into ['o', '', 'o|']).\n        if token:\n            # Look up the beat value for the current note token.\n            # As per SRS assumptions, input contains only valid note representations.\n            # If an invalid token were present, this would raise a KeyError.\n            beats_list.append(note_to_beats_map[token])\n\n    return beats_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/17", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n# Internal Map: A dictionary for efficient lookup of note types to beat durations.\n# Defined as a module-level constant for efficiency and adherence to \"constant dictionary\".\n_NOTE_BEAT_MAP = {\n    'o': 4,\n    'o|': 2,\n    '.|': 1\n}\n\ndef parse_music(music_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string of musical notes and returns a list of their beat durations.\n\n    Parameters:\n        music_string (str): A string containing space-separated musical notes.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of integers, where each integer is the beat duration\n                   for a corresponding note in the input string.\n\n    Preconditions:\n        - music_string must be a valid string.\n        - All note tokens within music_string must conform to the defined note types\n          ('o', 'o|', '.|'). Invalid tokens (e.g., '', 'x', etc.) are not explicitly\n          handled as per the design document and will result in a KeyError.\n          An empty input string \"\" will cause a KeyError as `\"\".split(' ')`\n          results in `['']` and ' ' is not a valid note token.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Tokenization: Split the input music_string into individual note tokens.\n    # The delimiter for splitting is a single space character.\n    note_tokens = music_string.split(' ')\n\n    # Iterate and Map: Retrieve beat durations for each token and collect them\n    # into a list using a list comprehension for conciseness and efficiency.\n    beat_durations = [_NOTE_BEAT_MAP[token] for token in note_tokens]\n\n    # Return the resulting list of beat durations.\n    return beat_durations"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/17", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef parse_music(music_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a specialized ASCII representation of musical notes into a list\n    of integers representing the beat duration of each note.\n\n    The function recognizes three types of notes:\n    - 'o': A whole note, lasting 4 beats.\n    - 'o|': A half note, lasting 2 beats.\n    - '.|': A quarter note, lasting 1 beat.\n\n    Notes in the input string should be separated by spaces. Unrecognized note\n    formats are silently skipped. An empty input string or a string with\n    only whitespace will result in an empty list.\n\n    Args:\n        music_string (str): A string containing space-separated musical note\n                            representations.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of integers, where each integer corresponds to the\n                   beat duration of a parsed note, in the order they appeared.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> parse_music('o o| .| o| o| .| .| .| .| o o')\n        [4, 2, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 4]\n        >>> parse_music('o')\n        [4]\n        >>> parse_music('o| .|')\n        [2, 1]\n        >>> parse_music('')\n        []\n        >>> parse_music('   ')\n        []\n        >>> parse_music('o invalid_note .| another_invalid')\n        [4, 1]\n    \"\"\"\n    note_beats_map = {\n        'o': 4,   # Whole note\n        'o|': 2,  # Half note\n        '.|': 1   # Quarter note\n    }\n\n    result_beats: List[int] = []\n\n    # Split the input string into individual note tokens.\n    # str.split() without arguments handles multiple spaces and\n    # leading/trailing spaces correctly, and produces an empty list if\n    # the string is empty or contains only whitespace.\n    note_tokens = music_string.split()\n\n    for token in note_tokens:\n        if token in note_beats_map:\n            beat_value = note_beats_map[token]\n            result_beats.append(beat_value)\n        # If the token is not found in the map, it is an unrecognized note\n        # and is silently skipped, as per the design specification.\n\n    return result_beats\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts all occurrences of a specified substring within a string,\n    correctly accounting for overlapping matches.\n\n    Args:\n        string (str): The larger text to be searched.\n        substring (str): The pattern whose occurrences are to be counted.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: A non-negative integer representing the total number of\n             overlapping occurrences.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    len_sub = len(substring)\n    len_str = len(string)\n\n    # Handle edge cases\n    # An empty substring has no meaningful \"overlapping occurrences\"\n    if len_sub == 0:\n        return 0\n    # If the string is shorter than the substring, it cannot contain it\n    if len_str < len_sub:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through all possible starting positions for the substring.\n    # The loop runs from index 0 up to (len_str - len_sub) inclusive.\n    # range(start, end) goes up to end-1, so we need (len_str - len_sub + 1)\n    for i in range(len_str - len_sub + 1):\n        # Extract a slice of 'string' of 'substring' length, starting at 'i'.\n        current_slice = string[i : i + len_sub]\n        \n        # Compare the extracted slice with the target substring.\n        if current_slice == substring:\n            count += 1\n            \n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts how many times a given substring can be found in the original string,\n    including overlapping cases.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The main string to search within.\n        substring: The substring to search for.\n\n    Returns:\n        The number of times the substring appears in the string,\n        including overlapping occurrences.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> how_many_times('', 'a')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('aaa', 'a')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('aaaa', 'aa')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('aaaaa', 'aaa')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('abcabc', 'abc')\n        2\n        >>> how_many_times('python', '')\n        0\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    len_string = len(string)\n    len_substring = len(substring)\n\n    # FR-003: Empty Substring Handling\n    if len_substring == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR-004: Empty Main String Handling\n    if len_string == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR-005: Substring Longer Than Main String\n    if len_substring > len_string:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through the string, considering all possible starting positions\n    # for an overlapping match. The loop range ensures that\n    # string[i : i + len_substring] is always a valid slice.\n    for i in range(len_string - len_substring + 1):\n        if string[i : i + len_substring] == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of times a given substring appears within an original string,\n    including overlapping occurrences.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The main string to be searched.\n        substring: The pattern to search for.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the total count of substring occurrences.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> how_many_times(\"banana\", \"ana\")\n        2\n        >>> how_many_times(\"aaaa\", \"aa\")\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times(\"abc\", \"d\")\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times(\"\", \"a\")\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times(\"abc\", \"\")\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times(\"abc\", \"abcd\")\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times(\"abababa\", \"aba\")\n        3\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    n = len(string)\n    m = len(substring)\n\n    # FR3: Handle Empty Main String\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR4: Handle Empty Substring\n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR5: Handle Substring Longer Than String\n    if m > n:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR1 & FR2: Iterate and count all occurrences, including overlapping ones\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        if string[i : i + m] == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of overlapping occurrences of a substring within a string.\n\n    This function finds all occurrences of the 'substring' in the 'string',\n    including cases where matches overlap.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The original text to be searched.\n        substring: The sequence of characters to find.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the total count of overlapping occurrences.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> how_many_times('aaaa', 'aa')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('aaa', 'a')\n        3\n        >>> how_many_times('abcde', 'xyz')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('', 'a')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('a', 'aa')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('abc', '')\n        0\n        >>> how_many_times('banana', 'ana')\n        2\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    n = len(string)\n    m = len(substring)\n\n    # FR5: Handling Empty Substring\n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # FR3 & FR4: Handling Empty Original String or Substring Longer Than String\n    # This also covers the case where string is empty, and substring is not.\n    if n < m:\n        return 0\n\n    start_index = 0\n    while True:\n        idx = string.find(substring, start_index)\n        if idx == -1:\n            # No more occurrences found\n            break\n        else:\n            # Match found, increment count\n            count += 1\n            # Advance search index by 1 to allow for overlapping matches\n            start_index = idx + 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "def how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of overlapping occurrences of a substring within a given string.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The main text being searched.\n        substring: The pattern being searched for.\n\n    Returns:\n        The total number of overlapping occurrences found.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n\n    # Handle empty substring as per SRS assumption (return 0)\n    if not substring:\n        return 0\n\n    # Optimization: If substring is longer than the main string,\n    # no matches are possible.\n    if len(substring) > len(string):\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through the main string to check for substring matches.\n    # The loop runs from index 0 up to (len(string) - len(substring)) inclusive.\n    # This ensures that string[i : i + len(substring)] always creates a\n    # slice of the correct length without going out of bounds.\n    for i in range(len(string) - len(substring) + 1):\n        # Check if the current slice of 'string' matches 'substring'\n        if string[i : i + len(substring)] == substring:\n            count += 1  # Increment counter for each match found\n    \n    return count  # Return the total count\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    n = len(string)\n    m = len(substring)\n\n    # FR4: Empty Substring Handling\n    if m == 0:\n        if n == 0:\n            return 1  # TC-009: Both empty strings\n        else:\n            return 0  # TC-008: Non-empty string, empty substring\n\n    count = 0\n    # FR1 & FR2: Count Substring Occurrences, Handle Overlapping Occurrences\n    # Iterate from 0 up to n - m (inclusive)\n    # This range ensures string[i : i + m] is always a valid slice of length m\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        # FR5: Case-Sensitive Matching\n        if string[i : i + m] == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "def how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the occurrences of a given substring within a larger string,\n    specifically addressing overlapping cases.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The main input text.\n        substring: The pattern to search for within the string.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the total number of times substring is found in string,\n        including all overlapping instances.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    n = len(string)\n    m = len(substring)\n\n    # FR6: If substring is an empty string (''), the function shall return 0.\n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n    # FR5: If string is an empty string (''), the function shall return 0.\n    # FR7: If substring is longer than string, the function shall return 0.\n    # These two are covered by the loop range for i, but explicit check for n == 0\n    # is cleaner before m > n check if we consider the edge case where m=1, n=0.\n    # Or simply, if m > n, the loop range (0 to n-m) will be empty if n-m < 0.\n    # But a direct check for m > n covers FR7 explicitly.\n    if n == 0 or m > n:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate with a sliding window. 'i' represents the starting index of the potential match.\n    # The loop runs from 0 up to n - m (inclusive) because the last possible start of\n    # the substring is when its end aligns with the end of the main string.\n    # E.g., if n=5, m=3, loop i from 0 to 5-3=2.\n    # i=0: string[0:3]\n    # i=1: string[1:4]\n    # i=2: string[2:5]\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        current_slice = string[i : i + m]\n        if current_slice == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts every instance where `substring` can be found within `string`,\n    including all overlapping occurrences.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The larger string to search within.\n        substring: The substring to count occurrences of.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the total number of overlapping occurrences.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    n = len(string)\n    m = len(substring)\n\n    # F.4 Empty Substring Input\n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # F.3 Empty String Input\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # F.5 Substring Longer than String\n    if m > n:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through the string to find overlapping occurrences\n    # The loop runs from index 0 up to n - m (inclusive)\n    # This ensures that there are enough characters remaining in 'string'\n    # to form a 'substring' match.\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        if string[i : i + m] == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts all occurrences of a specified substring within a given string,\n    including cases where these occurrences overlap.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    start_index = 0\n\n    # FR4: Empty Substring Handling\n    # FR5: Empty String Handling\n    # FR6: Substring Longer than String\n    if not substring or not string or len(substring) > len(string):\n        return 0\n\n    while True:\n        found_index = string.find(substring, start_index)\n        if found_index == -1:\n            break\n        \n        count += 1\n        start_index = found_index + 1\n        \n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/18", "solution": "```python\ndef how_many_times(main_string: str, substring: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of times a given substring occurs within a main string,\n    including overlapping occurrences.\n\n    Args:\n        main_string: The primary string in which occurrences are to be counted.\n        substring: The sequence of characters to be searched for within the main string.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the total number of times the substring appears\n        in the main string, including overlapping instances.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    n = len(main_string)\n    m = len(substring)\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-004: Handle Empty Substring\n    # REQ-FUNC-003: Handle Empty Main String (covered by m == 0 or loop range)\n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-005: Handle Substring Longer Than Main String\n    if m > n:\n        return 0\n\n    # Iterate through all possible starting positions for the substring\n    # The loop runs from 0 up to n - m (inclusive)\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        # Extract a slice of main_string with the length of substring\n        current_slice = main_string[i : i + m]\n        \n        # Compare the slice with the substring\n        if current_slice == substring:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/19", "solution": "```python\nWORD_TO_NUM_MAP = {\n    'zero': 0,\n    'one': 1,\n    'two': 2,\n    'three': 3,\n    'four': 4,\n    'five': 5,\n    'six': 6,\n    'seven': 7,\n    'eight': 8,\n    'nine': 9\n}\n\nNUM_TO_WORD_MAP = [\n    'zero',\n    'one',\n    'two',\n    'three',\n    'four',\n    'five',\n    'six',\n    'seven',\n    'eight',\n    'nine'\n]\n\ndef sort_numbers(numbers: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input is a space-delimited string of numberals from 'zero' to 'nine'.\n    Valid choices are 'zero', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight' and 'nine'.\n    Return the string with numbers sorted from smallest to largest\n    >>> sort_numbers('three one five')\n    'one three five'\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Split Input: Split the input string into individual number word strings.\n    word_list = numbers.split(' ')\n\n    # 2. Convert to Numbers: Map each number word to its corresponding integer value.\n    num_list = [WORD_TO_NUM_MAP[word] for word in word_list]\n\n    # 3. Sort Numbers: Sort the list of integers in ascending order.\n    num_list.sort()\n\n    # 4. Convert to Words: Map each sorted integer value back to its number word representation.\n    sorted_word_list = [NUM_TO_WORD_MAP[num] for num in num_list]\n\n    # 5. Join Output: Join the list of sorted number word strings into a single, space-delimited string.\n    output_string = ' '.join(sorted_word_list)\n\n    # 6. Return: Return the final sorted string.\n    return output_string\n\n```"}
