{"task_id": "HumanEval/19", "solution": "```python\nNUM_WORD_TO_INT_MAP = {\n    \"zero\": 0, \"one\": 1, \"two\": 2, \"three\": 3, \"four\": 4,\n    \"five\": 5, \"six\": 6, \"seven\": 7, \"eight\": 8, \"nine\": 9\n}\n\nINT_TO_NUM_WORD_MAP = [\n    \"zero\", \"one\", \"two\", \"three\", \"four\",\n    \"five\", \"six\", \"seven\", \"eight\", \"nine\"\n]\n\ndef sort_numbers(numbers: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a space-delimited string of number words ('zero' to 'nine')\n    into their numerical equivalents, sorts them from smallest to largest,\n    and then converts them back into a space-delimited string of sorted\n    number words.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A string containing one or more valid number words\n                 ('zero' through 'nine'), separated by single spaces.\n                 An empty string is also a valid input.\n\n    Returns:\n        A string containing the input number words, sorted numerically\n        from smallest to largest, separated by single spaces.\n        Returns an empty string if the input is an empty string.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle the empty string case as per acceptance criteria\n    if not numbers:\n        return ''\n\n    # 1. Input Parser: Split the input string into a list of individual number word strings.\n    word_list = numbers.split(' ')\n\n    # 2. Word-to-Int Mapper: Look up corresponding integer values.\n    int_list = [NUM_WORD_TO_INT_MAP[word] for word in word_list]\n\n    # 3. Sorter: Sort the list of integer values in ascending numerical order.\n    int_list.sort()\n\n    # 4. Int-to-Word Mapper: Look up corresponding number word strings.\n    sorted_word_list = [INT_TO_NUM_WORD_MAP[num] for num in int_list]\n\n    # 5. Output Formatter: Join the resulting list of sorted number word strings\n    #    back into a single string, using a single space as a delimiter.\n    sorted_numbers_string = ' '.join(sorted_word_list)\n\n    return sorted_numbers_string\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/19", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\nWORD_TO_INT_MAP = {\n    'zero': 0, 'one': 1, 'two': 2, 'three': 3, 'four': 4,\n    'five': 5, 'six': 6, 'seven': 7, 'eight': 8, 'nine': 9\n}\n\nINT_TO_WORD_LIST = [\n    'zero', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four',\n    'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight', 'nine'\n]\n\n\ndef sort_numbers(numbers: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input is a space-delimited string of numberals from 'zero' to 'nine'.\n    Valid choices are 'zero', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight' and 'nine'.\n    Return the string with numbers sorted from smallest to largest\n    >>> sort_numbers('three one five')\n    'one three five'\n    \"\"\"\n    word_list = numbers.split(' ')\n\n    number_list = []\n    for word in word_list:\n        number_list.append(WORD_TO_INT_MAP[word])\n\n    number_list.sort()\n\n    sorted_word_list = []\n    for number in number_list:\n        sorted_word_list.append(INT_TO_WORD_LIST[number])\n\n    return ' '.join(sorted_word_list)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/20", "solution": "```python\ndef find_closest_elements(numbers: list[float]) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the two numbers in a list that are closest to each other.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floats with at least two elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple (smaller_number, larger_number) of the two closest numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # 1. Sort the input list.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # 2. Initialize variables to track the minimum difference and the corresponding pair.\n    min_diff = float('inf')\n    closest_pair = (0.0, 0.0)\n\n    # 3. Iterate through adjacent elements in the sorted list.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        num1 = sorted_numbers[i]\n        num2 = sorted_numbers[i+1]\n        current_diff = num2 - num1  # Calculate difference (always non-negative)\n\n        # 4. Update min_diff and closest_pair if a smaller difference is found.\n        if current_diff < min_diff:\n            min_diff = current_diff\n            closest_pair = (num1, num2)\n\n            # 5. Optimization: If the minimum difference is 0, no smaller difference is possible.\n            # Return immediately to avoid unnecessary further comparisons.\n            if min_diff == 0.0:\n                return closest_pair\n\n    # 6. Return the closest pair found after iterating through all adjacent elements.\n    return closest_pair\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/20", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\ndef find_closest_elements(numbers: List[float]) -> Tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the two closest numbers within a given list of floating-point numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers. Guaranteed to have at least two elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple containing the two numbers from the input list that are closest to each other,\n        ordered as (smaller_number, larger_number).\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Sorting: Create a sorted copy of the input list.\n    sorted_numbers: List[float] = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # 2. Initialization:\n    # Initialize min_diff to a very large number.\n    min_diff: float = float('inf')\n    # Initialize closest_pair with dummy values; it will be updated in the first iteration.\n    closest_pair: Tuple[float, float] = (0.0, 0.0)\n\n    # 3. Iterate and Compare:\n    # Iterate through the sorted list, comparing adjacent elements.\n    # The loop goes up to the second-to-last element (index len(sorted_numbers) - 2)\n    # to always have an i+1 element for comparison.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        n1 = sorted_numbers[i]\n        n2 = sorted_numbers[i + 1]\n\n        # Calculate the difference between adjacent elements.\n        # Since the list is sorted, n2 >= n1, so absolute value is not needed.\n        current_diff = n2 - n1\n\n        # Comparison: If the current difference is smaller than min_diff, update.\n        if current_diff < min_diff:\n            min_diff = current_diff\n            closest_pair = (n1, n2)\n\n    # 4. Return Value:\n    return closest_pair\n```"}
