{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "import math\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest number that divides 'n' evenly and is smaller than 'n'.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): A positive integer greater than 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The largest proper divisor of 'n'.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If 'n' is less than or equal to 1.\n        TypeError: If 'n' is not an integer.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(4)\n        2\n        >>> largest_divisor(1)\n        Traceback (most recent call last):\\\n            ...\\\n        ValueError: Input 'n' must be a positive integer greater than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    # ERR-LD-001: Input value validation\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # PERF-LD-001: Efficient search up to sqrt(n)\n    # math.isqrt is available from Python 3.8+ and is efficient for integer square roots.\n    # For older Python versions, int(n**0.5) could be used.\n    limit = math.isqrt(n)\n\n    # REQ-LD-004: Iterate to find the smallest factor 'i'\n    # The corresponding largest proper divisor will be n // i\n    for i in range(2, limit + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return n // i\n\n    # REQ-LD-003: If no factors were found in the loop, 'n' must be a prime number.\n    # In this case, its largest proper divisor is 1.\n    return 1"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the largest positive integer that divides the given input integer 'n' evenly,\n    while also being strictly smaller than 'n'.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer (n >= 2).\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the largest proper divisor of 'n'.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If 'n' is less than or equal to 1, as no proper divisor exists.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(2)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(100)\n        50\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Iterate downwards from n // 2 to 1.\n    # The first divisor found will be the largest proper divisor.\n    for i in range(n // 2, 0, -1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return i\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the largest proper divisor of a given integer n.\n\n    A proper divisor is a divisor of a number, excluding the number itself.\n    For prime numbers, the largest proper divisor is 1.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer for which to find the largest proper divisor.\n                 Must be an integer greater than 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The largest proper divisor of n.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If n is not an integer.\n        ValueError: If n is an integer but n <= 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(12)\n        6\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(2)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR3: Input Validation - Type\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n\n    # FR3: Input Validation - Value\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # FR1, NFR1: Special Case: Even Number Optimization\n    # If n is even, its largest proper divisor is n // 2.\n    # This also handles n=2, returning 1 (FR2).\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return n // 2\n\n    # FR1, NFR1: General Case: Odd Numbers (Optimized Search)\n    # Start checking odd divisors from 3 up to sqrt(n).\n    # If we find a divisor `i`, then `n // i` is also a divisor.\n    # Since we are iterating `i` upwards, the first `i` found will be the\n    # smallest prime factor. Consequently, `n // i` will be the largest\n    # proper divisor.\n    # Using math.isqrt for integer square root for performance and clarity.\n    limit = math.isqrt(n)\n    for i in range(3, limit + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return n // i\n\n    # FR2: Prime Number Case\n    # If the loop completes without finding any divisors, it means n is prime.\n    # For prime numbers, the largest proper divisor is 1.\n    return 1\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates and returns the largest proper divisor of a given integer n.\n\n    A proper divisor is a positive divisor of a number, other than the number itself.\n\n    Args:\n        n: An integer greater than 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the largest proper divisor of n.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than or equal to 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input `n` must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of n.\n    # If i is a divisor, then n // i is also a divisor.\n    # The smallest factor (other than 1) will yield the largest proper divisor (n // i).\n    limit = int(n**0.5)\n    for i in range(2, limit + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # i is the smallest prime factor (or smallest factor > 1) found so far.\n            # n // i will be the largest proper divisor.\n            return n // i\n\n    # If the loop completes, n has no divisors between 2 and sqrt(n).\n    # This means n is a prime number (since n > 1 is already checked).\n    # For a prime number, the only proper divisor is 1.\n    return 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the largest proper divisor of a given positive integer 'n'.\n\n    A proper divisor is any divisor of 'n' other than 'n' itself.\n    If 'n' is a prime number, its largest proper divisor is 1.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer for which to find the largest proper divisor.\n                 Must be a positive integer greater than 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The largest proper divisor of 'n'. Returns 1 if 'n' is prime.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If 'n' is less than or equal to 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(12)\n        6\n        >>> largest_divisor(4)\n        2\n        >>> largest_divisor(2)\n        1\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the integer square root of n.\n    # If n has a divisor greater than its square root, it must also have\n    # a corresponding divisor smaller than its square root.\n    # The smallest factor 'i' found will correspond to the largest proper\n    # divisor n // i.\n    limit = math.isqrt(n)\n\n    for i in range(2, limit + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # Found the smallest prime factor 'i'.\n            # The largest proper divisor is n divided by this smallest factor.\n            return n // i\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means no divisors were found between 2 and sqrt(n).\n    # This implies that 'n' is a prime number.\n    # For prime numbers, the largest proper divisor is 1.\n    return 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly, strictly smaller than n.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer for which to find the largest proper divisor.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The largest integer divisor of n that is strictly smaller than n.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than or equal to 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(2)\n        1\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Iterate downwards from n-1 to 1\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        # Check if i divides n evenly\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # If it does, i is the largest divisor smaller than n\n            return i\n    \n    # This part of the code is logically unreachable for n > 1\n    # because 1 is always a divisor of any positive integer n,\n    # and the loop goes down to 1. However, as a safety measure\n    # or to satisfy static analysis for paths, it could be considered.\n    # Given the problem's constraints (n > 1) and algorithm, this will not be hit.\n    # For n=2, i will be 1, 2%1 == 0, returns 1.\n    # For n=7, i will be 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, then 1. 7%1 == 0, returns 1.\n    # For n=15, i will be 14, 13, ..., 6, then 5. 15%5 == 0, returns 5.\n    # Therefore, we are guaranteed to return from within the loop.\n    # A return here would imply an unexpected state, but it's not needed.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest positive integer that divides a given integer `n` evenly,\n    and is strictly smaller than `n`.\n\n    Args:\n        n: The input integer for which the largest proper divisor is sought.\n           Must be an integer greater than or equal to 2.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the largest proper divisor of `n`.\n        If `n` is a prime number, the function returns 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If `n` is less than 2.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(36)\n        18\n        >>> largest_divisor(2)\n        1\n        >>> # largest_divisor(1) # This would raise a ValueError\n    \"\"\"\n    # REQ-5: Input validation for n < 2\n    if n < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer greater than or equal to 2.\")\n\n    # REQ-3: Initialize max_divisor to 1. This handles prime numbers correctly,\n    # as 1 is their largest proper divisor.\n    max_divisor = 1\n\n    # PERF-1: Optimize by iterating only up to the square root of n.\n    # If 'i' is a divisor of 'n', then 'n // i' is also a divisor.\n    # One of these (i or n // i) will be less than or equal to sqrt(n),\n    # and the other will be greater than or equal to sqrt(n).\n    # By finding 'i' up to sqrt(n), we can identify the corresponding 'n // i'\n    # which could be the largest proper divisor.\n\n    # Calculate the upper limit for the loop. math.isqrt() is preferred for\n    # integer square root for Python 3.8+. Add 1 to ensure the loop\n    # correctly includes `floor(sqrt(n))` if it's an integer.\n    limit = math.isqrt(n) + 1\n\n    # Loop from 2 up to 'limit' (exclusive).\n    for i in range(2, limit):\n        # Check if 'i' is a divisor of 'n'.\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # If 'i' is a divisor, then 'n // i' is also a divisor.\n            # This 'other_divisor' is a candidate for the largest proper divisor.\n            # We take the maximum to ensure we always keep track of the largest one found so far.\n            other_divisor = n // i\n            max_divisor = max(max_divisor, other_divisor)\n\n    # REQ-2, REQ-3, REQ-4: Return the largest proper divisor found.\n    # If no divisor 'i' was found in the loop (meaning 'n' is prime),\n    # `max_divisor` remains 1.\n    return max_divisor\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly,\n    while being strictly smaller than n.\n\n    This function calculates the largest proper divisor (LPD) of a positive integer n.\n    A proper divisor is a divisor of a number, other than the number itself.\n\n    Args:\n        n: An integer. Must be greater than 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        The largest integer `d` such that `d < n` and `n % d == 0`.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If `n` is less than or equal to 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> largest_divisor(15)\n        5\n        >>> largest_divisor(7)\n        1\n        >>> largest_divisor(8)\n        4\n        >>> largest_divisor(2)\n        1\n        >>> # largest_divisor(1) # Raises ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n        >>> # largest_divisor(0) # Raises ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n        >>> # largest_divisor(-5) # Raises ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of n (inclusive).\n    # If n has any divisor i (where i >= 2), then n / i is also a divisor.\n    # To find the largest proper divisor, we look for the smallest factor 'i'.\n    # The corresponding factor 'n // i' will then be the largest proper divisor.\n    # We only need to check up to sqrt(n) because if n has a factor greater than sqrt(n),\n    # it must also have a factor smaller than sqrt(n) (or equal to it).\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # We found the smallest divisor 'i' (other than 1).\n            # The largest proper divisor will be 'n // i'.\n            return n // i\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means n has no divisors between 2 and sqrt(n).\n    # Since n > 1 was already validated, this implies n is a prime number.\n    # For prime numbers, the largest proper divisor is 1.\n    return 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    # FR.3, FR.4: Input Validation\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Optimized Algorithm\n    # 1. Handle even numbers (FR.1)\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return n // 2\n\n    # 2. Iterate for odd factors up to sqrt(n) (FR.1, NFR.1)\n    # The smallest factor `i` implies `n // i` is the largest factor < n\n    limit = int(math.sqrt(n))\n    for i in range(3, limit + 1, 2): # Check odd numbers\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return n // i\n\n    # 3. If no factors found, n is prime (FR.2)\n    return 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the largest proper divisor of a given positive integer n.\n\n    A proper divisor d of n is an integer such that n % d == 0 and d < n.\n    If n is a prime number, its only proper divisor is 1, so the function returns 1.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer (n >= 2).\n\n    Returns:\n        The largest proper divisor of n.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If n is not an integer.\n        ValueError: If n is less than 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 3.1.1 F1: Input Acceptance, 2.4 Constraints: Input Type\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n\n    # 2.4 Constraints: Input Range\n    if n < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer greater than or equal to 2.\")\n\n    # Algorithm: Optimized Factor Finding (from System Design 3.1 Component: largest_divisor Function)\n    # 3.1.2 F2: Divisor Check\n    # 3.1.3 F3: Proper Divisor Condition\n    # 3.1.4 F4: Largest Proper Divisor Selection\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the integer square root of n.\n    # If n has any factor, it must have one less than or equal to its square root.\n    # The first factor 'i' found will be the smallest factor (greater than 1).\n    # Consequently, 'n // i' will be the largest proper divisor.\n    for i in range(2, math.isqrt(n) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            # Found the smallest factor 'i', so 'n // i' is the largest proper divisor.\n            # This handles F6: Composite Number Handling.\n            return n // i\n    \n    # If the loop completes, no factors were found between 2 and sqrt(n).\n    # This implies n is a prime number (as n >= 2 is guaranteed by validation).\n    # According to 3.1.5 F5: Prime Number Handling, the largest proper divisor is 1.\n    return 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a list of prime factors of a given integer `n` in ascending order.\n    Each factor appears as many times as it contributes to the factorization.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer to be factorized. Must be > 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of prime factors.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than or equal to 1.\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    factors = []\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Handle odd factors\n    # We only need to check up to sqrt(n)\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2 # Move to the next odd number\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the prime factors of a given integer.\n\n    This function takes an integer 'n' as input and returns an ordered list\n    of its prime factors. Each factor appears in the list as many times as\n    it divides the input number.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer for which prime factors are to be determined.\n                 Must be a positive integer (n >= 1).\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: An ordered list of prime factors.\n                   Returns an empty list for n = 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> factorize(8)\n        [2, 2, 2]\n        >>> factorize(25)\n        [5, 5]\n        >>> factorize(70)\n        [2, 5, 7]\n        >>> factorize(1)\n        []\n        >>> factorize(13)\n        [13]\n        >>> factorize(123456789)\n        [3, 3, 3607, 3803]\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer greater than or equal to 1.\")\n    \n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    factors: List[int] = []\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Handle odd factors starting from 3\n    # We only need to check up to the square root of n\n    # and increment by 2 (checking only odd numbers)\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it means the remaining n is a prime factor itself\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the prime factorization of a given positive integer.\n\n    The function takes an integer n as input and returns a list of its prime\n    factors in ascending order, with each factor listed according to its multiplicity.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer to factorize. Must be a positive integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of prime factors of n, ordered from smallest to largest.\n                   Returns an empty list for n=1.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input n is not an integer.\n        ValueError: If the input n is 0 or a negative integer.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> factorize(8)\n        [2, 2, 2]\n        >>> factorize(25)\n        [5, 5]\n        >>> factorize(70)\n        [2, 5, 7]\n        >>> factorize(1)\n        []\n        >>> factorize(100)\n        [2, 2, 5, 5]\n        >>> factorize(97) # Prime number\n        [97]\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.REQ.004 - Handling of Invalid Input (Type Error)\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n\n    # F.REQ.004 - Handling of Invalid Input (Value Error)\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer greater than zero.\")\n\n    # F.REQ.003 - Handling of Input n=1\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    prime_factors: List[int] = []\n    temp_n = n\n\n    # Algorithm Step 3: Divide by 2\n    # Repeatedly divide by 2 until temp_n is odd\n    while temp_n % 2 == 0:\n        prime_factors.append(2)\n        temp_n //= 2\n\n    # Algorithm Step 4: Divide by Odd Numbers\n    # Start from 3 and increment by 2 (checking only odd numbers)\n    divisor = 3\n    # We only need to check divisors up to sqrt(temp_n)\n    while divisor * divisor <= temp_n:\n        # Repeatedly divide by the current divisor\n        while temp_n % divisor == 0:\n            prime_factors.append(divisor)\n            temp_n //= divisor\n        divisor += 2 # Move to the next odd number\n\n    # Algorithm Step 5: Handle Remaining Factor\n    # If temp_n is still greater than 1 after the loop, it means temp_n itself\n    # is a prime factor (and it must be a prime number larger than sqrt(original_n))\n    if temp_n > 1:\n        prime_factors.append(temp_n)\n\n    # F.REQ.002 - Output Format: The factors are collected in ascending order\n    # naturally by the algorithm's process.\n    return prime_factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the prime factorization of a given positive integer.\n\n    This function identifies all prime numbers that, when multiplied together,\n    yield the input integer `n`. Each prime factor appears in the list\n    as many times as it divides `n`. The factors are returned in ascending order.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The positive integer to be factorized.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of the prime factors of `n`, sorted in ascending order.\n                   Returns an empty list if `n` is 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If `n` is less than or equal to 0, as prime factorization\n                    is typically defined for positive integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Handle Edge Cases and Invalid Inputs\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer.\")\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    # 2. Initialize\n    factors = []\n\n    # 3. Factor by 2s\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # 4. Factor by Odd Divisors\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2 # Increment by 2 to check only odd numbers\n\n    # 5. Handle Remaining n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself.\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    # 6. Return factors\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\ndef factorize(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Factorizes a positive integer into its prime components.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The positive integer to be factorized.\n\n    Returns:\n        list[int]: A list of prime factors, sorted in ascending order,\n                   including multiplicities. Returns an empty list for n=1.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer (n >= 1)\")\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    factors = []\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Handle odd factors\n    divisor = 3\n    # Optimization: only check divisors up to the square root of n\n    while divisor * divisor <= n:\n        while n % divisor == 0:\n            factors.append(divisor)\n            n //= divisor\n        divisor += 2  # Increment by 2 to check only odd numbers\n\n    # Handle remaining factor (if any)\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it means the remaining n is a prime number\n    # greater than sqrt(original_n)\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    factors = []\n\n    # FR6.1: Handle edge cases for n = 0 or n = 1\n    if n <= 1:\n        return factors\n\n    # Step 3: Factor out 2s\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Step 4: Factor out odd numbers\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= n:\n        while n % i == 0:\n            factors.append(i)\n            n //= i\n        i += 2\n\n    # Step 5: Handle remaining prime (if n is a prime greater than 2)\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    # FR4, FR5: Factors are automatically ordered and include multiplicity due to the algorithm\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer to factorize. Must be an integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of prime factors, sorted in ascending order.\n                   Returns an empty list for n <= 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> factorize(8)\n        [2, 2, 2]\n        >>> factorize(25)\n        [5, 5]\n        >>> factorize(70)\n        [2, 5, 7]\n        >>> factorize(1)\n        []\n        >>> factorize(0)\n        []\n        >>> factorize(-10)\n        []\n        >>> factorize(101)\n        [101]\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n\n    # Handle edge cases (n <= 1)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return factors\n\n    # Divide by 2 until n is odd\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Divide by odd numbers from 3 up to sqrt(n)\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2 # Check only odd numbers\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Computes the prime factorization of a given positive integer.\n\n    This function takes an integer `n` as input and returns a list of its\n    prime factors, ordered from smallest to largest, with each factor\n    appearing according to its multiplicity.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer for which to compute the prime factorization.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers representing the prime factors of `n`, sorted\n        in ascending order, with multiplicity. Returns an empty list for `n=1`.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If `n` is less than 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> factorize(8)\n        [2, 2, 2]\n        >>> factorize(25)\n        [5, 5]\n        >>> factorize(70)\n        [2, 5, 7]\n        >>> factorize(1)\n        []\n        >>> factorize(17)\n        [17]\n        >>> factorize(0)\n        Traceback (most recent call last):\n            ...\n        ValueError: Input must be a positive integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer.\")\n    \n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    factors: List[int] = []\n\n    # Factor out any factors of 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Factor out odd numbers starting from 3\n    # We only need to check up to the square root of n\n    # because if n has a prime factor p > sqrt(n),\n    # then n must also have a prime factor q < sqrt(n).\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2 # Increment by 2 to check only odd numbers\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it means the remaining n is a prime number\n    # (and larger than any factor already found).\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the prime factorization of a given integer.\n\n    This function takes an integer 'n' and returns a list of its prime factors\n    in ascending order, including their correct multiplicities.\n\n    For example:\n    - factorize(12) returns [2, 2, 3]\n    - factorize(70) returns [2, 5, 7]\n    - factorize(1) returns []\n    - factorize(0) returns []\n    - factorize(-5) returns []\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer for which prime factors are to be computed.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list containing the prime factors of 'n' in ascending order.\n                   Returns an empty list for n <= 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n\n    # FR-2: Handle edge cases for n <= 1\n    if n <= 1:\n        return []\n\n    # Step 3: Divide by 2\n    # This handles all occurrences of 2 as a prime factor\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Step 4: Divide by odd numbers starting from 3\n    # We only need to check odd divisors up to sqrt(n)\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2  # Increment by 2 to check only odd numbers\n\n    # Step 5: Handle remaining factor (if any)\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime number itself\n    # (and larger than sqrt of the original n, or it was the original n if prime)\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    # FR-5, FR-6: Return the list of factors.\n    # The algorithm naturally adds factors in ascending order.\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the prime factorization of a given integer n using trial division.\n\n    Args:\n        n: The integer for which prime factors are to be found.\n           Must be a positive integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of prime factors, sorted smallest to largest, with multiplicity.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is less than or equal to 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n\n    # 1. Handle Edge Cases\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Prime factorization is not defined for non-positive integers.\")\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    # 2. Factor by 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # 3. Factor by Odd Numbers\n    divisor = 3\n    # Loop while divisor * divisor <= n to optimize checking up to sqrt(n)\n    while divisor * divisor <= n:\n        while n % divisor == 0:\n            factors.append(divisor)\n            n //= divisor\n        divisor += 2  # Increment by 2 to check only odd numbers\n\n    # 4. Handle Remaining Prime Factor\n    # If n is still greater than 1 after the loop, it must be a prime factor itself.\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    # 5. Return Result\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom typing import List\nfrom collections import Counter\n\napp = FastAPI(\n    title=\"Duplicate Remover Service\",\n    description=\"API to remove duplicate elements from a list of integers, preserving order.\",\n    version=\"1.0.0\",\n)\n\nclass NumbersRequest(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"\n    Request model for the numbers list.\n    \"\"\"\n    numbers: List[int]\n\nclass NumbersResponse(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"\n    Response model for the processed numbers list.\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[int]\n\ndef remove_duplicates_logic(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes elements that occur more than once from a list of integers,\n    keeping only those that appear exactly once and preserving their original order.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing only the unique elements (appearing exactly once)\n        from the input list, maintaining their relative order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    # Step 1: Count frequencies of each number in a single pass\n    counts = Counter(numbers)\n\n    # Step 2: Iterate through the original list again to build the result\n    # only appending numbers whose count is exactly 1.\n    result = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if counts[num] == 1:\n            result.append(num)\n            \n    return result\n\n@app.post(\n    \"/remove-duplicates\",\n    response_model=NumbersResponse,\n    summary=\"Remove duplicate integers from a list\",\n    description=\"Processes a list of integers, returning a new list containing \"\n                \"only those elements that appear exactly once, while preserving \"\n                \"their original order of appearance.\",\n    responses={\n        200: {\n            \"description\": \"Successfully processed the list and returned unique elements.\",\n            \"content\": {\n                \"application/json\": {\n                    \"example\": {\"result\": [3, 4, 5]}\n                }\n            }\n        },\n        400: {\n            \"description\": \"Invalid input format or data.\",\n            \"content\": {\n                \"application/json\": {\n                    \"example\": {\"detail\": \"Input must be a valid list of integers.\"}\n                }\n            }\n        },\n        500: {\n            \"description\": \"Internal server error.\",\n            \"content\": {\n                \"application/json\": {\n                    \"example\": {\"detail\": \"Internal Server Error: Something went wrong.\"}\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n)\nasync def process_numbers(request: NumbersRequest) -> NumbersResponse:\n    \"\"\"\n    Endpoint to remove duplicate numbers from a list.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        processed_numbers = remove_duplicates_logic(request.numbers)\n        return NumbersResponse(result=processed_numbers)\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Catch unexpected errors during processing and return a 500 status.\n        # FastAPI's Pydantic handles validation errors (e.g., non-int in list)\n        # by returning 422 Unprocessable Entity automatically.\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=f\"Internal Server Error: {str(e)}\")\n\n# To run this application:\n# 1. Save it as a Python file (e.g., main.py).\n# 2. Install FastAPI and Uvicorn: pip install fastapi uvicorn\n# 3. Run from your terminal: uvicorn main:app --reload\n# 4. Access the API documentation at http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef remove_duplicates(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes all elements from a list of integers that occur more than once.\n    Keeps the order of the remaining elements the same as in the input.\n    \"\"\"\n    # First Pass (Counting Occurrences):\n    # Initialize a hash map (dictionary) to store the frequency of each number.\n    counts: dict[int, int] = {}\n    for number in numbers:\n        counts[number] = counts.get(number, 0) + 1\n\n    # Second Pass (Filtering and Building Result):\n    # Initialize an empty list to store the elements that appear exactly once.\n    result: List[int] = []\n    # Iterate through the original numbers list again to preserve order.\n    for number in numbers:\n        # If the count of the current number is 1, it means it appeared exactly once.\n        if counts[number] == 1:\n            result.append(number)\n\n    # Return the new list containing only the unique elements in their original relative order.\n    return result\n```"}
